1
00:00:05,480 --> 00:00:07,960
Today I'm going to cover primarily preference

2
00:00:07,960 --> 00:00:09,100
optimization

3
00:00:09,100 --> 00:00:12,440
And I think Alvaro is going to cover some things related

4
00:00:12,440 --> 00:00:13,960
to reasoning

5
00:00:13,960 --> 00:00:19,840
So let's get started

6
00:00:19,840 --> 00:00:24,300
I guess the one clear trend in the last 5 to 10 years

7
00:00:24,300 --> 00:00:25,880
has been that we've been like building

8
00:00:25,880 --> 00:00:28,920
larger and larger models, and we've

9
00:00:28,920 --> 00:00:31,380
been putting far more compute into them

10
00:00:31,380 --> 00:00:34,680
This is like an ever very consistent trend

11
00:00:34,680 --> 00:00:39,000
And if you're familiar with-- or if you've-- some people are even

12
00:00:39,000 --> 00:00:41,780
making predictions about AGI 2027

13
00:00:41,780 --> 00:00:44,320
and they are predicting that we're going to keep putting more

14
00:00:44,320 --> 00:00:49,040
and more floating-point operations into these models

15
00:00:49,040 --> 00:00:52,120
But the question today we're going to try to answer

16
00:00:52,120 --> 00:00:55,720
is, how do we get from these large language models, which

17
00:00:55,720 --> 00:00:58,760
are pretrained to predict text and get

18
00:00:58,760 --> 00:01:01,090
these really nice chatbots that some of them

19
00:01:01,090 --> 00:01:02,970
you might have used

20
00:01:02,970 --> 00:01:06,210
So I'm going to very briefly talk about--

21
00:01:06,210 --> 00:01:08,570
this is not an NLP course, and you should probably

22
00:01:08,570 --> 00:01:10,610
take one if you're interested in learning more

23
00:01:10,610 --> 00:01:11,430
about pre-training

24
00:01:11,430 --> 00:01:13,050
It's a very, very deep topic

25
00:01:13,050 --> 00:01:15,170
But I'm going to quickly touch upon what

26
00:01:15,170 --> 00:01:19,250
we learn in pre-training and why that doesn't necessarily

27
00:01:19,250 --> 00:01:23,330
translate into the kind of chatbots we use

28
00:01:23,330 --> 00:01:27,030
So in pretraining large language models

29
00:01:27,030 --> 00:01:28,770
they're trained on these gigantic corpus

30
00:01:28,770 --> 00:01:30,250
of internet texts

31
00:01:30,250 --> 00:01:32,850
And the way they're trained is roughly

32
00:01:32,850 --> 00:01:36,130
predicting what the next word or the next token in the sentence

33
00:01:36,130 --> 00:01:37,330
is going to be

34
00:01:37,330 --> 00:01:40,590
So, for example, if you have a sentence

35
00:01:40,590 --> 00:01:44,330
Stanford University is located in blank, California

36
00:01:44,330 --> 00:01:47,690
So the model will learn to predict what this text is

37
00:01:47,690 --> 00:01:48,570
And you can imagine

38
00:01:48,570 --> 00:01:51,490
Then you're learning to predict this text

39
00:01:51,490 --> 00:01:52,790
You're learning a lot of facts

40
00:01:52,790 --> 00:01:54,110
You're learning a lot of trivia

41
00:01:54,110 --> 00:01:55,900
But you're also starting to learn something

42
00:01:55,900 --> 00:02:00,450
about the syntax of how language operates

43
00:02:00,450 --> 00:02:03,710
And we can look at many different examples here

44
00:02:03,710 --> 00:02:05,590
I went to the kitchen to make some tea

45
00:02:05,590 --> 00:02:08,150
Standing next to Iroh, Zuko pondered his destiny

46
00:02:08,150 --> 00:02:10,330
Zuko left the-- so yeah

47
00:02:10,330 --> 00:02:13,730
The model will learn to predict the next token, which implicitly

48
00:02:13,730 --> 00:02:17,250
requires it to know some facts, know syntax, and also maybe

49
00:02:17,250 --> 00:02:21,130
even reason about what word should come next

50
00:02:21,130 --> 00:02:24,270
While this may like predicting next token, you learn language

51
00:02:24,270 --> 00:02:28,390
but predicting next token ends up learning a lot more

52
00:02:28,390 --> 00:02:30,170
And there's some evidence that it ends up

53
00:02:30,170 --> 00:02:33,290
learning some notion of how the world operates

54
00:02:33,290 --> 00:02:36,770
So this is a very nice example where

55
00:02:36,770 --> 00:02:41,070
we ask the model to predict the next set of tokens

56
00:02:41,070 --> 00:02:44,390
And here, the example is, Pat watches a demonstration

57
00:02:44,390 --> 00:02:45,810
of a bowling ball and a leaf being

58
00:02:45,810 --> 00:02:48,690
dropped at the same time in a vacuum chamber

59
00:02:48,690 --> 00:02:50,370
Pat, who is a physicist, predicts

60
00:02:50,370 --> 00:02:54,010
that another model is asked to predict the next set of tokens

61
00:02:54,010 --> 00:02:55,710
or the next set of words here

62
00:02:55,710 --> 00:02:57,630
And to predict these next set of words

63
00:02:57,630 --> 00:03:00,720
you implicitly have to understand what the text was

64
00:03:00,720 --> 00:03:02,860
And in some way, you have to understand

65
00:03:02,860 --> 00:03:06,020
how the world operates as well to be able to answer

66
00:03:06,020 --> 00:03:08,040
And it's very interesting

67
00:03:08,040 --> 00:03:10,580
If you change the prompt, you'll get a different behavior out

68
00:03:10,580 --> 00:03:12,180
of the model as well

69
00:03:12,180 --> 00:03:14,900
The point here being that language models don't just

70
00:03:14,900 --> 00:03:16,920
learn syntax, don't just learn words

71
00:03:16,920 --> 00:03:20,620
but they learn a lot more about how the world operates as well

72
00:03:20,620 --> 00:03:24,100
And similarly, we can ask them to do math questions

73
00:03:24,100 --> 00:03:26,980
We can ask them to generate code

74
00:03:26,980 --> 00:03:29,580
We can ask them-- they have fairly reasonable

75
00:03:29,580 --> 00:03:31,580
medical understanding as well

76
00:03:31,580 --> 00:03:35,740
And more importantly, we can start transforming these models

77
00:03:35,740 --> 00:03:39,700
into assistants now

78
00:03:39,700 --> 00:03:43,140
So the question now we'll get back to is like

79
00:03:43,140 --> 00:03:44,960
OK, pre-training gave us all that

80
00:03:44,960 --> 00:03:49,300
So how do we actually make this into assistants

81
00:03:49,300 --> 00:03:54,100
So today's lecture, we'll try to cover roughly two or three kinds

82
00:03:54,100 --> 00:03:55,700
of techniques to do so

83
00:03:55,700 --> 00:03:57,670
First is instruction fine tuning

84
00:03:57,670 --> 00:03:59,350
Second is RLHF

85
00:03:59,350 --> 00:04:00,640
And third is DPO

86
00:04:00,640 --> 00:04:02,390
And then we'll talk about some limitations

87
00:04:02,390 --> 00:04:07,790
about what is missing, where are we going wrong, and so on

88
00:04:07,790 --> 00:04:09,110
All right

89
00:04:09,110 --> 00:04:12,590
So the first thing to understand is when we pretrain models

90
00:04:12,590 --> 00:04:14,050
we're modeling language

91
00:04:14,050 --> 00:04:16,089
And yes, that learns more than language

92
00:04:16,089 --> 00:04:17,930
It learns something about the world

93
00:04:17,930 --> 00:04:20,529
But that's still not equivalent to assisting users

94
00:04:20,529 --> 00:04:25,450
So this is an example from GPT-3, where the prompt says

95
00:04:25,450 --> 00:04:28,570
explain the moon landing to a six-year-old in a few sentences

96
00:04:28,570 --> 00:04:30,710
And the model completes it with what

97
00:04:30,710 --> 00:04:32,590
it thinks should be the next set of tokens

98
00:04:32,590 --> 00:04:35,990
according to the distribution of text it has seen in its corpus

99
00:04:35,990 --> 00:04:37,950
So it starts off, explain the theory of gravity

100
00:04:37,950 --> 00:04:39,070
to a six-year-old

101
00:04:39,070 --> 00:04:41,150
Explain the theory of relativity to a six-year-old

102
00:04:41,150 --> 00:04:42,530
in a few sentences

103
00:04:42,530 --> 00:04:44,550
And this isn't like--

104
00:04:44,550 --> 00:04:46,450
this is not what I expect my assistant to do

105
00:04:46,450 --> 00:04:50,910
It's just a language model predicting the next token

106
00:04:50,910 --> 00:04:53,150
What we do want it to do is actually

107
00:04:53,150 --> 00:04:56,450
give an explanation or a story where it like

108
00:04:56,450 --> 00:04:58,990
a giant rocket ship blasted off the Earth carrying astronauts

109
00:04:58,990 --> 00:04:59,570
to the moon

110
00:04:59,570 --> 00:05:01,630
So some kind of an explanation, which

111
00:05:01,630 --> 00:05:04,210
was the original intent of the question

112
00:05:04,210 --> 00:05:06,870
and not just the text that has seen on the internet

113
00:05:06,870 --> 00:05:10,230
And that's where the discrepancy arises between language modeling

114
00:05:10,230 --> 00:05:12,950
and assisting users

115
00:05:12,950 --> 00:05:15,430
The first set of techniques that were developed for this

116
00:05:15,430 --> 00:05:16,670
was fine-tuning

117
00:05:16,670 --> 00:05:19,230
And we're going to talk a little bit about this

118
00:05:19,230 --> 00:05:23,970
So the general paradigm, even to this date

119
00:05:23,970 --> 00:05:26,270
has been to first pretrain a language model

120
00:05:26,270 --> 00:05:28,910
on a large corpus of text and then fine-tune

121
00:05:28,910 --> 00:05:31,690
for your specific task

122
00:05:31,690 --> 00:05:33,530
So how does fine-tuning work

123
00:05:33,530 --> 00:05:36,970
You collect a set of inputs, and you have some target outputs

124
00:05:36,970 --> 00:05:39,310
And then you improve the log likelihood

125
00:05:39,310 --> 00:05:41,190
of these target outputs

126
00:05:41,190 --> 00:05:45,810
So in this step two, you have this input

127
00:05:45,810 --> 00:05:48,470
which was, the movie was something, something

128
00:05:48,470 --> 00:05:52,050
And then you have some target output, which is good or bad

129
00:05:52,050 --> 00:05:54,880
And the model learns to predict whether the output was

130
00:05:54,880 --> 00:05:56,000
good or bad

131
00:05:56,000 --> 00:06:00,640
And that's really the main difference between fine-tuning

132
00:06:00,640 --> 00:06:04,000
and pre-training

133
00:06:04,000 --> 00:06:05,280
As we realize--

134
00:06:05,280 --> 00:06:08,860
I mean, these pre-trained models are good for many more things

135
00:06:08,860 --> 00:06:12,580
We started scaling up the number of tasks we can fine-tune on

136
00:06:12,580 --> 00:06:14,760
So the previous example was maybe

137
00:06:14,760 --> 00:06:16,650
something like sentiment prediction

138
00:06:16,650 --> 00:06:18,400
But really, these models can be fine-tuned

139
00:06:18,400 --> 00:06:23,600
for many, many different tasks at the same time

140
00:06:23,600 --> 00:06:29,800
So broadly, this can be seen as collecting a set of instruction

141
00:06:29,800 --> 00:06:31,200
and output pairs

142
00:06:31,200 --> 00:06:33,080
So for example, if the instruction is, please

143
00:06:33,080 --> 00:06:34,420
answer the following question

144
00:06:34,420 --> 00:06:36,920
What is the boiling point of nitrogen

145
00:06:36,920 --> 00:06:39,760
we'll have some answer like minus 320.4

146
00:06:39,760 --> 00:06:42,200
And we want to be able to predict this answer conditioned

147
00:06:42,200 --> 00:06:44,160
on the input

148
00:06:44,160 --> 00:06:48,680
And once we collect a corpus of such instruction output pairs

149
00:06:48,680 --> 00:06:52,870
we can then evaluate them on many, many unseen tasks

150
00:06:52,870 --> 00:06:54,490
Can Geoffrey Hinton have a conversation

151
00:06:54,490 --> 00:06:55,790
with George Washington

152
00:06:55,790 --> 00:06:57,570
Give the rationale before answering

153
00:06:57,570 --> 00:07:03,770
And the model will produce a reasonable output here

154
00:07:03,770 --> 00:07:09,530
So what if we fine-tune different scale language models

155
00:07:09,530 --> 00:07:11,810
on the same kind of corpus

156
00:07:11,810 --> 00:07:15,530
So one thing that was observed in a lot of prior work

157
00:07:15,530 --> 00:07:18,690
is that as you increase the model parameter size

158
00:07:18,690 --> 00:07:20,910
they actually start generalizing a lot better

159
00:07:20,910 --> 00:07:24,290
So even taking a very small corpus of such instruction

160
00:07:24,290 --> 00:07:26,810
output data sets, you start seeing

161
00:07:26,810 --> 00:07:28,350
a lot more generalization

162
00:07:28,350 --> 00:07:30,970
So to explain the table a little bit

163
00:07:30,970 --> 00:07:35,330
the BIG-bench and MMLU are like question-answer pairs

164
00:07:35,330 --> 00:07:37,930
of the kind you just saw in the previous slide

165
00:07:37,930 --> 00:07:43,610
And one thing to note here is that the bigger the parameters

166
00:07:43,610 --> 00:07:46,970
or larger the model is, the larger the gain is

167
00:07:46,970 --> 00:07:50,090
after instruction fine-tuning

168
00:07:50,090 --> 00:07:56,130
So what this suggest to us is that the models start responding

169
00:07:56,130 --> 00:07:59,050
and generalizing to questions, even with very small corpus

170
00:07:59,050 --> 00:08:01,090
of data as well

171
00:08:01,090 --> 00:08:04,450
So to give an example of what the models look before and after

172
00:08:04,450 --> 00:08:06,220
instruction fine-tuning--

173
00:08:06,220 --> 00:08:07,470
it's a fairly simple question

174
00:08:07,470 --> 00:08:10,170
In the following sentences, explain the antecedent

175
00:08:10,170 --> 00:08:12,270
to the pronoun and state that it is ambiguous

176
00:08:12,270 --> 00:08:15,330
So you get something which represents more

177
00:08:15,330 --> 00:08:19,210
like the raw language model, which will start generating

178
00:08:19,210 --> 00:08:20,710
text on the right side

179
00:08:20,710 --> 00:08:23,210
The reporter and the chef will discuss their favorite dishes

180
00:08:23,210 --> 00:08:23,890
and so on

181
00:08:23,890 --> 00:08:26,110
But after instruction fine-tuning

182
00:08:26,110 --> 00:08:28,730
it will give us what the user perhaps

183
00:08:28,730 --> 00:08:32,010
intended from the question, which

184
00:08:32,010 --> 00:08:37,090
is to give the correct answer

185
00:08:37,090 --> 00:08:38,530
Cool

186
00:08:38,530 --> 00:08:42,409
So instruction fine-tuning is fairly simple

187
00:08:42,409 --> 00:08:47,650
There is nothing very complicated going on here

188
00:08:47,650 --> 00:08:49,890
So I'll take a second

189
00:08:49,890 --> 00:08:52,440
Do you see-- just reflect for yourself

190
00:08:52,440 --> 00:08:56,340
Do you see some limitations to something like this

191
00:08:56,340 --> 00:08:58,780
One limitation that obviously comes up

192
00:08:58,780 --> 00:09:01,120
is that it's expensive to collect such data

193
00:09:01,120 --> 00:09:04,700
You have to collect questions and the labels

194
00:09:04,700 --> 00:09:06,900
for those questions

195
00:09:06,900 --> 00:09:08,000
And that's hard

196
00:09:08,000 --> 00:09:10,280
As you start increasing the hardness of the questions

197
00:09:10,280 --> 00:09:13,300
there's very few people who can answer those questions

198
00:09:13,300 --> 00:09:16,940
So it's expensive to scale such data collections where

199
00:09:16,940 --> 00:09:18,940
the humans have to sit down and write down

200
00:09:18,940 --> 00:09:22,020
the answer to every question

201
00:09:22,020 --> 00:09:24,720
But then if you look beyond that

202
00:09:24,720 --> 00:09:28,260
there are certain tasks which don't even have a right answer

203
00:09:28,260 --> 00:09:31,580
So if I-- and this is a lot of the use

204
00:09:31,580 --> 00:09:32,760
cases of language models

205
00:09:32,760 --> 00:09:35,500
A lot of the use cases of language models are creative

206
00:09:35,500 --> 00:09:37,860
So if I ask a language model to write a story

207
00:09:37,860 --> 00:09:40,920
about a dog and her pet grasshopper

208
00:09:40,920 --> 00:09:42,600
there's no right answer for this

209
00:09:42,600 --> 00:09:45,420
I cannot sit down and say, oh, this is the exact story I want

210
00:09:45,420 --> 00:09:52,110
to hear every single time a person asks this question

211
00:09:52,110 --> 00:09:54,330
The second point is a little subtle

212
00:09:54,330 --> 00:10:00,210
but in instruction fine-tuning, we have a predefined answer

213
00:10:00,210 --> 00:10:04,130
So the mistakes are penalized equally

214
00:10:04,130 --> 00:10:06,350
So there's no good or right answer

215
00:10:06,350 --> 00:10:10,070
We're not necessarily saying that this was the mistake

216
00:10:10,070 --> 00:10:12,870
We are basically saying your entire answer is wrong

217
00:10:12,870 --> 00:10:15,290
or your entire answer is correct

218
00:10:15,290 --> 00:10:21,470
which is often not necessarily true for a specific question

219
00:10:21,470 --> 00:10:23,750
So this is an example of this

220
00:10:23,750 --> 00:10:28,150
If we have a statement like, Avatar is a fantasy TV show

221
00:10:28,150 --> 00:10:30,150
it's not that the sentence entirely

222
00:10:30,150 --> 00:10:33,230
is correct or incorrect, but it would have been better

223
00:10:33,230 --> 00:10:36,510
to describe the Avatar as an adventure TV show

224
00:10:36,510 --> 00:10:39,270
rather than a fantasy TV show

225
00:10:39,270 --> 00:10:41,810
I think this is like a problem that has emerged recently

226
00:10:41,810 --> 00:10:45,270
is that the language models truly are better at generating

227
00:10:45,270 --> 00:10:47,470
answers than humans

228
00:10:47,470 --> 00:10:50,310
So if we rely on humans to generate the answers

229
00:10:50,310 --> 00:10:52,290
and generate the labels for our questions

230
00:10:52,290 --> 00:10:55,590
we are going to be limited by how good the humans can

231
00:10:55,590 --> 00:10:58,150
answer the question

232
00:10:58,150 --> 00:11:04,470
And finally, one big mismatch is that LLM objectives--

233
00:11:04,470 --> 00:11:07,310
there's a bit of a misalignment between what

234
00:11:07,310 --> 00:11:09,950
we want our models to do, which is to engage well

235
00:11:09,950 --> 00:11:12,920
with humans, which is to--

236
00:11:12,920 --> 00:11:16,550
and which is to follow their intent

237
00:11:16,550 --> 00:11:20,190
Whereas our fine-tuning objective basically says

238
00:11:20,190 --> 00:11:22,330
predict this answer really well

239
00:11:22,330 --> 00:11:24,870
So there's a little bit of a mismatch in our training

240
00:11:24,870 --> 00:11:28,790
objective when we are doing instruction fine-tuning

241
00:11:28,790 --> 00:11:31,350
Cool

242
00:11:31,350 --> 00:11:35,910
So this leads us to the paradigm of reinforcement learning

243
00:11:35,910 --> 00:11:38,430
from human preferences

244
00:11:38,430 --> 00:11:43,170
So the question is that, how much of the improvement

245
00:11:43,170 --> 00:11:45,590
from instruction fine-tuning comes from following a format

246
00:11:45,590 --> 00:11:48,240
or actually teaching some new knowledge

247
00:11:48,240 --> 00:11:52,000
I think most of the work suggests that it actually

248
00:11:52,000 --> 00:11:54,660
is teaching largely formatting

249
00:11:54,660 --> 00:11:56,080
And most of the knowledge actually

250
00:11:56,080 --> 00:11:58,180
comes from the base language model itself

251
00:11:58,180 --> 00:12:01,040
And there's a very clear way to do this

252
00:12:01,040 --> 00:12:03,680
You can experiment this by just training

253
00:12:03,680 --> 00:12:05,720
the model wrong answers

254
00:12:05,720 --> 00:12:09,780
And it still does quite well on the downstream tasks

255
00:12:09,780 --> 00:12:11,160
So it tells us that it's actually

256
00:12:11,160 --> 00:12:15,000
teaching formatting more than the knowledge itself

257
00:12:15,000 --> 00:12:19,840
The question was, are the corpuses always

258
00:12:19,840 --> 00:12:21,100
question-answer format

259
00:12:21,100 --> 00:12:23,720
Because that's what we want out of chatbots

260
00:12:23,720 --> 00:12:24,740
Not anymore

261
00:12:24,740 --> 00:12:28,020
I think this was very early days of LLM fine-tuning

262
00:12:28,020 --> 00:12:30,400
and we were focused more on question answering

263
00:12:30,400 --> 00:12:34,000
But the corpus for instruction fine-tuning

264
00:12:34,000 --> 00:12:36,560
are very, very diverse now

265
00:12:36,560 --> 00:12:40,320
These LLMs are used for a wide variety of tasks, function

266
00:12:40,320 --> 00:12:41,780
calling tools

267
00:12:41,780 --> 00:12:43,840
And it's not just assist--

268
00:12:43,840 --> 00:12:46,580
assistance means many different things

269
00:12:46,580 --> 00:12:48,030
It's not question-answers

270
00:12:48,030 --> 00:12:50,350
It could be just anything

271
00:12:50,350 --> 00:12:50,850
Great

272
00:12:50,850 --> 00:12:53,090
So now we'll get into reinforcement learning

273
00:12:53,090 --> 00:12:55,170
from human preferences, which addresses

274
00:12:55,170 --> 00:13:00,170
some of the limitations which we discussed here

275
00:13:00,170 --> 00:13:03,530
So throughout this section, we'll

276
00:13:03,530 --> 00:13:07,010
use summarization as an example, because there's not necessarily

277
00:13:07,010 --> 00:13:09,450
a good summary that we can often train on

278
00:13:09,450 --> 00:13:13,730
And for notation, we'll assume that we

279
00:13:13,730 --> 00:13:17,450
are given an instruction x and the language model

280
00:13:17,450 --> 00:13:19,490
will generate some sample y

281
00:13:19,490 --> 00:13:23,890
And we'll pretend that we have a way for a human

282
00:13:23,890 --> 00:13:26,850
to give us some kind of a reward score for a given

283
00:13:26,850 --> 00:13:30,890
summary conditioned on some input x and summary y

284
00:13:30,890 --> 00:13:33,290
So we have some news article which

285
00:13:33,290 --> 00:13:34,730
is San Francisco, California

286
00:13:34,730 --> 00:13:37,770
Magnitude of 4.2 earthquake shook the San Francisco

287
00:13:37,770 --> 00:13:39,090
blah, blah, blah

288
00:13:39,090 --> 00:13:41,650
And we have one summary, which is

289
00:13:41,650 --> 00:13:43,030
earthquake hit San Francisco

290
00:13:43,030 --> 00:13:45,890
There was a minor property damage but no injuries

291
00:13:45,890 --> 00:13:50,590
And we have another one, which is, Bay Area has good weather

292
00:13:50,590 --> 00:13:52,840
but it is prone to earthquakes and wildfires

293
00:13:52,840 --> 00:13:55,510
Clearly, one of the summaries is a little better than the other

294
00:13:55,510 --> 00:13:57,970
So let's say like our reward function gives us

295
00:13:57,970 --> 00:14:02,130
a score of 8.0, and the other summary gets a score of 1.2

296
00:14:02,130 --> 00:14:04,810
Roughly speaking, you want to set up the objective such

297
00:14:04,810 --> 00:14:09,030
that we want to maximize the expected reward

298
00:14:09,030 --> 00:14:12,370
So conditioned on some input instruction x

299
00:14:12,370 --> 00:14:15,850
we want our language model to generate summaries y such

300
00:14:15,850 --> 00:14:18,410
that the expected reward will be high

301
00:14:18,410 --> 00:14:20,810
Fairly simple standard RL objective that you've probably

302
00:14:20,810 --> 00:14:23,650
seen in the class before

303
00:14:23,650 --> 00:14:27,670
And this is like the core of how chatbots are trained

304
00:14:27,670 --> 00:14:31,330
And the rough pipeline looks as the following, which

305
00:14:31,330 --> 00:14:33,170
is from the InstructGPT paper, is

306
00:14:33,170 --> 00:14:36,650
that we'll first do some instruction fine-tuning

307
00:14:36,650 --> 00:14:38,970
This is we collect some demonstration data to condition

308
00:14:38,970 --> 00:14:42,890
like just to get the formatting right as we discussed earlier

309
00:14:42,890 --> 00:14:47,080
Second step and the third step, which will go a lot more into

310
00:14:47,080 --> 00:14:52,500
is to set up how to first get a reward model, which allows

311
00:14:52,500 --> 00:14:54,120
us to align with human intent

312
00:14:54,120 --> 00:14:56,940
And then we maximize the reward model

313
00:14:56,940 --> 00:15:00,860
So these are the three steps we typically follow

314
00:15:00,860 --> 00:15:02,140
Great

315
00:15:02,140 --> 00:15:07,020
So let's just pretend for now that we have a reward model

316
00:15:07,020 --> 00:15:08,940
where we want to maximize this

317
00:15:08,940 --> 00:15:13,300
And yeah, I'm presuming you all are

318
00:15:13,300 --> 00:15:16,260
familiar with this gradient descent idea

319
00:15:16,260 --> 00:15:19,900
So, yeah, the way we would change the LLM parameters

320
00:15:19,900 --> 00:15:22,420
to maximize this is something you've

321
00:15:22,420 --> 00:15:24,560
seen before, like some form of gradient descent

322
00:15:24,560 --> 00:15:26,960
Usually, there's more complicated optimizers

323
00:15:26,960 --> 00:15:31,140
but we can pretend that is not required right now

324
00:15:31,140 --> 00:15:33,980
For now, we'll just assume like a generic RL formulation

325
00:15:33,980 --> 00:15:37,620
where we are generating some sample S hat

326
00:15:37,620 --> 00:15:40,260
So for some distribution p theta S

327
00:15:40,260 --> 00:15:43,430
we want to change the parameter's theta such

328
00:15:43,430 --> 00:15:46,910
that the sample's S hat are high reward under the reward function

329
00:15:46,910 --> 00:15:50,190
r

330
00:15:50,190 --> 00:15:54,310
So the main issue is-- and this is why we do RL

331
00:15:54,310 --> 00:15:57,210
is our reward functions are often nondifferentiable

332
00:15:57,210 --> 00:15:58,630
So if you ask me to give a number

333
00:15:58,630 --> 00:16:00,930
my human mind is nondifferentiable

334
00:16:00,930 --> 00:16:02,690
So we cannot backpropagate through them

335
00:16:02,690 --> 00:16:06,310
So this is why we resort to RL

336
00:16:06,310 --> 00:16:08,230
So this is where policy gradient methods

337
00:16:08,230 --> 00:16:11,390
that I think Chelsea has covered very early in the course come

338
00:16:11,390 --> 00:16:13,190
in, is that they allow us to maximize

339
00:16:13,190 --> 00:16:15,310
nondifferentiable reward functions

340
00:16:15,310 --> 00:16:18,830
And we can estimate and optimize this objective

341
00:16:18,830 --> 00:16:22,410
And I'll do a brief recap here because it's important

342
00:16:22,410 --> 00:16:25,450
And we'll slowly lead into the RLHF pipeline as well

343
00:16:25,450 --> 00:16:29,750
But I presume you have seen this before

344
00:16:29,750 --> 00:16:31,130
Great

345
00:16:31,130 --> 00:16:35,070
So in this generic objective when we want to estimate

346
00:16:35,070 --> 00:16:39,950
the quantity that we want is the gradient of the expected reward

347
00:16:39,950 --> 00:16:44,670
where we want the sample's S hat to have a high reward under R

348
00:16:44,670 --> 00:16:47,510
So this can be written down

349
00:16:47,510 --> 00:16:51,110
This is just algebra at this point, where the expectation can

350
00:16:51,110 --> 00:16:56,470
be written down as the summation of R(s) multiplied by p theta S

351
00:16:56,470 --> 00:16:59,790
And we have the gradient here

352
00:16:59,790 --> 00:17:02,350
So this is just a definition of expectation

353
00:17:02,350 --> 00:17:06,650
And now we're going to propagate the gradient forward simply

354
00:17:06,650 --> 00:17:12,869
by linearity of the gradient

355
00:17:12,869 --> 00:17:15,490
I assume you have seen this, but I'm going to go over this

356
00:17:15,490 --> 00:17:17,329
But this is a log derivative trick

357
00:17:17,329 --> 00:17:20,829
which is we can rewrite the gradient of p theta

358
00:17:20,829 --> 00:17:25,390
as p theta times gradient of log p theta

359
00:17:25,390 --> 00:17:29,070
So to see that, this is simply from the chain rule

360
00:17:29,070 --> 00:17:32,670
is that the gradient of log p theta

361
00:17:32,670 --> 00:17:35,510
simply can be written as 1 by p theta and gradient multiplied

362
00:17:35,510 --> 00:17:38,630
by gradient of p theta

363
00:17:38,630 --> 00:17:39,670
Cool

364
00:17:39,670 --> 00:17:42,760
So we can rewrite this to get this identity, where

365
00:17:42,760 --> 00:17:45,240
the gradient of p theta can be rewritten as p

366
00:17:45,240 --> 00:17:49,480
theta gradient log p theta

367
00:17:49,480 --> 00:17:51,520
Cool

368
00:17:51,520 --> 00:17:57,560
So the quantities in yellow is what we want to substitute back

369
00:17:57,560 --> 00:18:00,280
And if we plug this back in, we get

370
00:18:00,280 --> 00:18:04,840
the following equation, which is reward of gradient of p theta

371
00:18:04,840 --> 00:18:07,920
is the same as expectation under p theta

372
00:18:07,920 --> 00:18:12,840
R gradient of log p theta

373
00:18:12,840 --> 00:18:13,920
All right

374
00:18:13,920 --> 00:18:17,720
So why did we do all this

375
00:18:17,720 --> 00:18:21,080
Typically, when we're computing expectation over sentences

376
00:18:21,080 --> 00:18:23,400
it's really not possible to enumerate

377
00:18:23,400 --> 00:18:26,440
every single sentence possible

378
00:18:26,440 --> 00:18:29,540
It's just simply intractable to even try to do that

379
00:18:29,540 --> 00:18:32,960
So you want to be able to estimate the gradient somehow

380
00:18:32,960 --> 00:18:34,960
And we want to be able to create some kind

381
00:18:34,960 --> 00:18:37,440
of a stochastic estimator so that we

382
00:18:37,440 --> 00:18:39,060
can get the gradient out

383
00:18:39,060 --> 00:18:41,250
So this log derivative trick is what

384
00:18:41,250 --> 00:18:44,290
allows us to take this gradient and convert it

385
00:18:44,290 --> 00:18:48,330
into an expectation, which we can sample and then approximate

386
00:18:48,330 --> 00:18:52,290
And this is what we get as a final result, is that it's again

387
00:18:52,290 --> 00:18:54,130
an expectation of a reward multiplied

388
00:18:54,130 --> 00:18:57,450
by gradient of log p theta

389
00:18:57,450 --> 00:19:00,810
So the question is, do we take this gradient because we cannot

390
00:19:00,810 --> 00:19:02,190
backpropagate correctly

391
00:19:02,190 --> 00:19:03,850
There's two reasons, really

392
00:19:03,850 --> 00:19:06,610
First is we cannot set up an objective where the reward--

393
00:19:06,610 --> 00:19:09,210
we cannot assume the reward is differentiable

394
00:19:09,210 --> 00:19:12,330
Second, which is the more precise mathematical answer

395
00:19:12,330 --> 00:19:13,410
is that we are--

396
00:19:13,410 --> 00:19:16,810
our parameters are in the expectation

397
00:19:16,810 --> 00:19:21,250
So we do need to figure out a way to take the gradients back

398
00:19:21,250 --> 00:19:24,930
to the parameters in the expectation as well

399
00:19:24,930 --> 00:19:25,690
Yeah

400
00:19:25,690 --> 00:19:28,170
But if our rewards were differentiable

401
00:19:28,170 --> 00:19:30,690
we don't necessarily need to set our optimization objective

402
00:19:30,690 --> 00:19:31,190
like this

403
00:19:31,190 --> 00:19:33,130
We could imagine other optimization objectives

404
00:19:33,130 --> 00:19:39,000
where we directly backpropagate end to end

405
00:19:39,000 --> 00:19:42,670
OK, so, yeah

406
00:19:42,670 --> 00:19:44,530
So we went through all the math just

407
00:19:44,530 --> 00:19:46,410
to show that we can rewrite the gradient

408
00:19:46,410 --> 00:19:48,210
of our original objective

409
00:19:48,210 --> 00:19:50,330
And we can rewrite it as an expectation

410
00:19:50,330 --> 00:19:53,170
again, which we can now approximate using Monte Carlo

411
00:19:53,170 --> 00:19:56,010
estimates, which is the equation on the end

412
00:19:56,010 --> 00:19:58,530
And we can take this approximate gradient

413
00:19:58,530 --> 00:20:01,210
and plug it into our favorite optimizer which uses

414
00:20:01,210 --> 00:20:04,370
gradient descent or ascent

415
00:20:04,370 --> 00:20:05,890
And that's really like--

416
00:20:05,890 --> 00:20:10,490
this is just supposed to be a brief recap, so yeah

417
00:20:10,490 --> 00:20:12,750
And it's a very intuitive objective as well

418
00:20:12,750 --> 00:20:18,170
So really, all it's saying is that if for a certain sample

419
00:20:18,170 --> 00:20:21,890
the reward is high, we should increase the log likelihood

420
00:20:21,890 --> 00:20:26,490
So the parameters are taking the gradient

421
00:20:26,490 --> 00:20:32,050
in the direction of samples which score high on the reward

422
00:20:32,050 --> 00:20:34,550
And that's really all this objective says

423
00:20:34,550 --> 00:20:36,730
And if it's bad, you take directions

424
00:20:36,730 --> 00:20:40,100
opposite to those samples

425
00:20:40,100 --> 00:20:41,100
Cool

426
00:20:41,100 --> 00:20:43,220
And yeah, this is why we call it reinforcement

427
00:20:43,220 --> 00:20:46,140
learning because we're doing good actions go there

428
00:20:46,140 --> 00:20:48,580
Bad actions don't go there

429
00:20:48,580 --> 00:20:52,100
The question is, am I drawing full sentences or tokens

430
00:20:52,100 --> 00:20:55,340
In this context, we'll be drawing full sentences

431
00:20:55,340 --> 00:20:56,260
Yeah

432
00:20:56,260 --> 00:20:57,840
The notation was set up generally

433
00:20:57,840 --> 00:21:00,220
so we'll go back to the xy notation again

434
00:21:00,220 --> 00:21:03,500
But that was just supposed to be a brief primer on policy

435
00:21:03,500 --> 00:21:06,380
gradients

436
00:21:06,380 --> 00:21:07,580
Cool

437
00:21:07,580 --> 00:21:12,900
So first problem is that we have so far

438
00:21:12,900 --> 00:21:15,180
assumed that we have some kind of a reward function

439
00:21:15,180 --> 00:21:18,360
which will tell us like, hey, the summary is 10.0

440
00:21:18,360 --> 00:21:21,660
The other is 5.0

441
00:21:21,660 --> 00:21:23,000
That usually is not possible

442
00:21:23,000 --> 00:21:24,940
Let's say you're training like the biggest

443
00:21:24,940 --> 00:21:26,450
and the greatest LLM possible

444
00:21:26,450 --> 00:21:28,240
And it needs like millions of such queries

445
00:21:28,240 --> 00:21:30,420
And you're not going to have a human army sit there

446
00:21:30,420 --> 00:21:32,380
and label every answer

447
00:21:32,380 --> 00:21:35,900
So it's not very scalable to ask humans directly

448
00:21:35,900 --> 00:21:39,310
So the general solution towards this end

449
00:21:39,310 --> 00:21:44,110
has been to make preference learning a separate optimization

450
00:21:44,110 --> 00:21:46,470
problem, where we try to figure out a human reward

451
00:21:46,470 --> 00:21:50,470
model before starting our RL optimization

452
00:21:50,470 --> 00:21:52,890
So, yeah

453
00:21:52,890 --> 00:21:54,390
So we had two summaries

454
00:21:54,390 --> 00:21:55,910
And we presented

455
00:21:55,910 --> 00:21:57,930
The score was 8.0 and 1.2

456
00:21:57,930 --> 00:22:00,670
And we'll try to train on some kind of data

457
00:22:00,670 --> 00:22:02,670
where we try to predict the score so that we

458
00:22:02,670 --> 00:22:05,430
have a learned reward function, which we can later

459
00:22:05,430 --> 00:22:08,120
use in the RL

460
00:22:08,120 --> 00:22:08,620
OK

461
00:22:08,620 --> 00:22:12,210
So we talked about we want to learn this reward model

462
00:22:12,210 --> 00:22:15,910
And we have some summaries where we

463
00:22:15,910 --> 00:22:17,670
want to be able to predict their score

464
00:22:17,670 --> 00:22:20,970
So how do we get these scores

465
00:22:20,970 --> 00:22:23,470
So we need some kind of an annotated data set

466
00:22:23,470 --> 00:22:28,030
Now the issue is that if we ask humans for scores directly

467
00:22:28,030 --> 00:22:29,950
two different humans have completely different

468
00:22:29,950 --> 00:22:31,190
calibration

469
00:22:31,190 --> 00:22:34,350
So for a summary that has a score of 7

470
00:22:34,350 --> 00:22:37,090
would mean something completely differently to two humans

471
00:22:37,090 --> 00:22:39,410
So there's not really an easy way to aggregate

472
00:22:39,410 --> 00:22:41,830
And even for the same human, they

473
00:22:41,830 --> 00:22:43,920
would probably score the same summary differently

474
00:22:43,920 --> 00:22:45,710
at different points, depending on what they

475
00:22:45,710 --> 00:22:47,390
ate for lunch or something

476
00:22:47,390 --> 00:22:52,510
So it's not really easy to get a score directly from humans

477
00:22:52,510 --> 00:22:56,410
And if you ask them directly, it doesn't work out very well

478
00:22:56,410 --> 00:22:57,870
The reward models you learn are not

479
00:22:57,870 --> 00:23:01,470
very useful for training such language models

480
00:23:01,470 --> 00:23:05,990
So the solution to this is to not actually ask them

481
00:23:05,990 --> 00:23:09,710
for a score, but to actually ask them for preferences

482
00:23:09,710 --> 00:23:14,650
So we give them few summaries, and we just ask them, hey

483
00:23:14,650 --> 00:23:17,530
do you prefer this summary over this summary or not

484
00:23:17,530 --> 00:23:19,710
And this is the kind of annotated data set we should

485
00:23:19,710 --> 00:23:23,310
expect to collect for humans

486
00:23:23,310 --> 00:23:26,870
So now going a little bit deeper into this

487
00:23:26,870 --> 00:23:30,150
so we are going to collect a data set of the form where we're

488
00:23:30,150 --> 00:23:31,890
given, some kind of an input x

489
00:23:31,890 --> 00:23:35,080
And we're going to be given some kind of a winning summary, which

490
00:23:35,080 --> 00:23:36,160
is yw

491
00:23:36,160 --> 00:23:39,440
And we're going to be given some kind of a losing summary, y L

492
00:23:39,440 --> 00:23:42,480
And we're going to have some data set D for this

493
00:23:42,480 --> 00:23:46,080
And what we want to do is train a reward model

494
00:23:46,080 --> 00:23:49,840
which assigns a higher score to the winning summaries

495
00:23:49,840 --> 00:23:53,640
and assigns a lower score to the losing summaries

496
00:23:53,640 --> 00:23:56,320
So the optimization objective, which

497
00:23:56,320 --> 00:23:58,480
is called the Bradley-Terry objective

498
00:23:58,480 --> 00:24:01,640
is a way of going from such data set of preferences

499
00:24:01,640 --> 00:24:04,320
to an implicit reward score, which would

500
00:24:04,320 --> 00:24:06,720
align with the preferences

501
00:24:06,720 --> 00:24:11,800
And the way we train this is we minimize this log sigmoid

502
00:24:11,800 --> 00:24:15,400
And the difference of reward is assigned to the winning

503
00:24:15,400 --> 00:24:17,080
and the losing summary

504
00:24:17,080 --> 00:24:22,160
So, yeah, all this objective is saying

505
00:24:22,160 --> 00:24:24,760
is that a reward model should make

506
00:24:24,760 --> 00:24:27,520
sure the reward for the correct summary

507
00:24:27,520 --> 00:24:31,920
is higher than the reward of the losing summary

508
00:24:31,920 --> 00:24:33,690
So the question is like, is it unbounded

509
00:24:33,690 --> 00:24:37,050
or do you normalize this

510
00:24:37,050 --> 00:24:39,340
The actual issue that arises, which is-- this

511
00:24:39,340 --> 00:24:41,010
is actually a very important observation

512
00:24:41,010 --> 00:24:45,130
that we'll actually use to develop DPO as well

513
00:24:45,130 --> 00:24:48,930
The objective just asks the difference to be high

514
00:24:48,930 --> 00:24:52,050
So the difference will be high even if you assign a reward

515
00:24:52,050 --> 00:24:53,950
of 1,000 and 10,000

516
00:24:53,950 --> 00:24:56,290
Or it'll be high even if you assign a difference

517
00:24:56,290 --> 00:24:58,290
from 1 to 999

518
00:24:58,290 --> 00:25:02,210
There's no real scaling here which allows that

519
00:25:02,210 --> 00:25:03,970
So, yeah, typically, you have to do

520
00:25:03,970 --> 00:25:05,530
some kind of post hoc normalization

521
00:25:05,530 --> 00:25:08,690
to make sure that different prompts get rewards

522
00:25:08,690 --> 00:25:10,490
at the same scale

523
00:25:10,490 --> 00:25:12,490
Otherwise, the optimization objective

524
00:25:12,490 --> 00:25:16,330
can get a little wonky

525
00:25:16,330 --> 00:25:19,030
But yeah, this is the Bradley-Terry objective

526
00:25:19,030 --> 00:25:21,770
which allows us to go from a data set of preferences

527
00:25:21,770 --> 00:25:25,530
to a reward model which we'll use in our lecture

528
00:25:25,530 --> 00:25:28,650
Yeah, the question is, is that why we take the sigmoid

529
00:25:28,650 --> 00:25:29,350
Not really

530
00:25:29,350 --> 00:25:33,770
There's a few different functions you can set up

531
00:25:33,770 --> 00:25:37,190
Sigmoid just maps naturally to like, oh

532
00:25:37,190 --> 00:25:40,690
the probability of this sample winning over this sample

533
00:25:40,690 --> 00:25:44,470
So, yeah, that's why it's typically used as a sigmoid

534
00:25:44,470 --> 00:25:46,090
But really, all we want to encourage

535
00:25:46,090 --> 00:25:47,730
is that the reward model should--

536
00:25:47,730 --> 00:25:51,490
the difference should be high

537
00:25:51,490 --> 00:25:53,090
All right

538
00:25:53,090 --> 00:25:54,950
So now we get back to our RL loop

539
00:25:54,950 --> 00:25:56,590
And what do we have

540
00:25:56,590 --> 00:25:59,370
We have some kind of an instruction fine-tuned model

541
00:25:59,370 --> 00:26:00,690
let's say, p PT

542
00:26:00,690 --> 00:26:03,410
We have learned some kind of a reward model using

543
00:26:03,410 --> 00:26:06,790
the Bradley-Terry objective, which gives us a scalar output

544
00:26:06,790 --> 00:26:09,970
So it takes an input, x, gives us a summary

545
00:26:09,970 --> 00:26:16,650
and then gives us a scalar output, RM phi x comma y

546
00:26:16,650 --> 00:26:18,370
OK, so this is the simple part

547
00:26:18,370 --> 00:26:20,950
In some ways, we're going to do the RLHF loop here

548
00:26:20,950 --> 00:26:22,770
And the way we're going to do this is we

549
00:26:22,770 --> 00:26:26,290
want to optimize the expected reward as we started with

550
00:26:26,290 --> 00:26:28,690
where we're going to plug in our LLM parameters

551
00:26:28,690 --> 00:26:31,660
in the expectation for p theta

552
00:26:31,660 --> 00:26:34,420
So it will produce some kind of a summary, y hat, conditioned

553
00:26:34,420 --> 00:26:35,900
on some input, x

554
00:26:35,900 --> 00:26:38,540
And our reward model is going to be the learned reward

555
00:26:38,540 --> 00:26:40,820
model from preferences

556
00:26:40,820 --> 00:26:45,420
Before I move on, I have a question which is slightly hard

557
00:26:45,420 --> 00:26:48,340
I started off saying that the reward functions are not

558
00:26:48,340 --> 00:26:49,700
differentiable

559
00:26:49,700 --> 00:26:52,420
And that's part of why we want to do RL

560
00:26:52,420 --> 00:26:55,260
But we just learned a reward model

561
00:26:55,260 --> 00:26:57,240
And we know--

562
00:26:57,240 --> 00:26:58,940
we typically learn a reward model using

563
00:26:58,940 --> 00:27:00,320
neural networks themselves

564
00:27:00,320 --> 00:27:02,360
And they are very much differentiable

565
00:27:02,360 --> 00:27:04,380
We can do backpropagate through them

566
00:27:04,380 --> 00:27:05,490
So why are we doing RL

567
00:27:05,490 --> 00:27:08,020
[INAUDIBLE] That's the right answer

568
00:27:08,020 --> 00:27:12,940
So the token-- like we're outputting discrete tokens

569
00:27:12,940 --> 00:27:15,860
And when we assume we can backpropagate things

570
00:27:15,860 --> 00:27:19,700
we cannot actually backpropagate through the discrete language

571
00:27:19,700 --> 00:27:21,420
tokens

572
00:27:21,420 --> 00:27:24,780
So even if we have a reward model that is differentiable

573
00:27:24,780 --> 00:27:26,160
we generate some output

574
00:27:26,160 --> 00:27:27,680
We plug it into our reward model

575
00:27:27,680 --> 00:27:29,180
And then we want to go back directly

576
00:27:29,180 --> 00:27:32,510
to the parameters of the language model

577
00:27:32,510 --> 00:27:35,670
But we get this discrete bottleneck in the middle

578
00:27:35,670 --> 00:27:38,870
where we cannot backpropagate our gradients from the reward

579
00:27:38,870 --> 00:27:42,270
model to the language model

580
00:27:42,270 --> 00:27:44,970
And that's the reason why we again have to resort to RL

581
00:27:44,970 --> 00:27:48,150
even though we have a differentiable non-black box

582
00:27:48,150 --> 00:27:49,790
reward model here

583
00:27:49,790 --> 00:27:51,270
But yeah, good attempts, everyone

584
00:27:51,270 --> 00:27:53,110
These are close

585
00:27:53,110 --> 00:27:54,580
Yeah

586
00:27:54,580 --> 00:27:56,870
So let's say like we have a reward

587
00:27:56,870 --> 00:28:01,230
model, RM phi x comma y hat

588
00:28:01,230 --> 00:28:04,600
Let's assume like y is a continuous variable

589
00:28:04,600 --> 00:28:06,810
So typically, we'll take some input

590
00:28:06,810 --> 00:28:08,090
We'll generate some output

591
00:28:08,090 --> 00:28:11,230
We'll plug it into our learn reward model

592
00:28:11,230 --> 00:28:14,990
And if everything is a continuous function here

593
00:28:14,990 --> 00:28:20,470
you can rely on backpropagation to take the gradients

594
00:28:20,470 --> 00:28:24,110
and update the model parameters such that the reward model

595
00:28:24,110 --> 00:28:25,750
score keeps going up

596
00:28:25,750 --> 00:28:27,570
So you don't actually need to do RL

597
00:28:27,570 --> 00:28:29,470
And not doing RL is often very good

598
00:28:29,470 --> 00:28:33,150
because it's expensive to do because of the sampling

599
00:28:33,150 --> 00:28:37,530
But the issue is that our y hat here are discrete tokens

600
00:28:37,530 --> 00:28:40,590
So they're sampled from some underlying distribution

601
00:28:40,590 --> 00:28:45,310
And once you include the sampling operation

602
00:28:45,310 --> 00:28:46,660
it's very hard to--

603
00:28:46,660 --> 00:28:50,070
there are tricks to do that, but they're not very stable

604
00:28:50,070 --> 00:28:53,230
But it's hard to backpropagate through that

605
00:28:53,230 --> 00:28:53,790
Yes

606
00:28:53,790 --> 00:28:56,270
So I didn't want to assume like Gumbel-SoftMax

607
00:28:56,270 --> 00:28:58,910
is known to people here, but Gumbel-SoftMax

608
00:28:58,910 --> 00:29:03,430
unfortunately, so far hasn't scaled super well, especially

609
00:29:03,430 --> 00:29:05,890
now the language-- so to contextualize this

610
00:29:05,890 --> 00:29:08,590
y hat is a sentence which could be thousands

611
00:29:08,590 --> 00:29:10,050
and thousands of tokens

612
00:29:10,050 --> 00:29:12,630
And each token vocabulary sizes are

613
00:29:12,630 --> 00:29:16,090
on the order of hundreds of thousands of tokens

614
00:29:16,090 --> 00:29:20,790
So yeah, the entire sentence is a combinatorial space of like

615
00:29:20,790 --> 00:29:24,910
100,000 tokens multiplied by the sequence length

616
00:29:24,910 --> 00:29:28,240
Gumbel-SoftMax usually doesn't scale beyond thousand dimensions

617
00:29:28,240 --> 00:29:29,480
or so on

618
00:29:29,480 --> 00:29:31,760
Yeah

619
00:29:31,760 --> 00:29:32,480
Cool

620
00:29:32,480 --> 00:29:34,800
This is a hard question, so I wanted

621
00:29:34,800 --> 00:29:38,200
to touch upon this because you should never

622
00:29:38,200 --> 00:29:41,840
assume that you need to do RL

623
00:29:41,840 --> 00:29:45,160
So yeah

624
00:29:45,160 --> 00:29:45,700
All right

625
00:29:45,700 --> 00:29:51,440
So one issue, even when we set up this objective

626
00:29:51,440 --> 00:29:53,620
is that-- and somebody alluded to this

627
00:29:53,620 --> 00:29:56,100
was that our reward models are never perfect

628
00:29:56,100 --> 00:30:00,340
So we don't want to assume that they'll give us a perfect score

629
00:30:00,340 --> 00:30:01,760
After all, they have been like fit

630
00:30:01,760 --> 00:30:04,140
to human preferences on a certain distribution

631
00:30:04,140 --> 00:30:05,640
We don't want to assume that they'll

632
00:30:05,640 --> 00:30:10,090
give us a good score for every single input and output

633
00:30:10,090 --> 00:30:13,920
And if you do Naive optimization like we described so far

634
00:30:13,920 --> 00:30:16,820
you will get models which have the reward model

635
00:30:16,820 --> 00:30:19,560
And you'll see some plots for that later as well

636
00:30:19,560 --> 00:30:22,000
So one another thing we add is something

637
00:30:22,000 --> 00:30:26,410
called the KL penalty or the Kullback-Leibler divergence

638
00:30:26,410 --> 00:30:32,010
And what the KL penalty basically says is--

639
00:30:32,010 --> 00:30:37,410
so it's added to the reward, where we have beta log p

640
00:30:37,410 --> 00:30:39,830
theta by p pre-trained

641
00:30:39,830 --> 00:30:43,830
So p theta is the quantity we are optimizing right now

642
00:30:43,830 --> 00:30:45,370
This is the language model, which

643
00:30:45,370 --> 00:30:49,210
is currently assigning the probabilities to token y hat

644
00:30:49,210 --> 00:30:51,730
And p pre-trained is the model we started with

645
00:30:51,730 --> 00:30:53,310
So it could be instruction fine-tuned

646
00:30:53,310 --> 00:30:55,850
And these are the probabilities under those model

647
00:30:55,850 --> 00:31:00,810
And roughly speaking, whenever a probability distribution

648
00:31:00,810 --> 00:31:04,570
deviates from the pre-trained distribution, we get a penalty

649
00:31:04,570 --> 00:31:07,850
So we get a lower score if we are deviating too much

650
00:31:07,850 --> 00:31:11,890
from the pre-trained model

651
00:31:11,890 --> 00:31:15,010
And if you work out the math, this thing

652
00:31:15,010 --> 00:31:19,930
is exactly the KL divergence between our distribution

653
00:31:19,930 --> 00:31:22,790
or our language model that we are optimizing right now

654
00:31:22,790 --> 00:31:26,620
and the language model we started with

655
00:31:26,620 --> 00:31:32,800
And the reason why we want to do this is that, as I said

656
00:31:32,800 --> 00:31:35,180
the reward model is imperfect

657
00:31:35,180 --> 00:31:38,340
We know it's more accurate on a distribution of preferences

658
00:31:38,340 --> 00:31:40,300
that we trained on, and we want to make

659
00:31:40,300 --> 00:31:45,340
sure the model stays close to where it started off from

660
00:31:45,340 --> 00:31:48,920
So this is one of the first examples of RLHF

661
00:31:48,920 --> 00:31:52,520
where it worked quite well

662
00:31:52,520 --> 00:31:54,980
So this is a summarization task itself

663
00:31:54,980 --> 00:31:57,980
And the curve basically--

664
00:31:57,980 --> 00:32:00,460
the x-axis is the model size

665
00:32:00,460 --> 00:32:03,940
And the y-axis is, how often is the summary

666
00:32:03,940 --> 00:32:09,980
preferred over some ground truth summary for some news articles

667
00:32:09,980 --> 00:32:11,520
And if you look at the blue curve

668
00:32:11,520 --> 00:32:13,460
it's the pre-training curve, which

669
00:32:13,460 --> 00:32:16,740
as the model size increases, the fraction of summaries

670
00:32:16,740 --> 00:32:19,500
preferred over the baseline increases

671
00:32:19,500 --> 00:32:22,480
And then you have SFT or instruction fine-tuning

672
00:32:22,480 --> 00:32:26,520
which gives an improvement over the pre-trained model

673
00:32:26,520 --> 00:32:28,520
But when you look at the RL curve

674
00:32:28,520 --> 00:32:31,260
this is the first model which actually exceeds

675
00:32:31,260 --> 00:32:34,660
the human written summaries

676
00:32:34,660 --> 00:32:36,620
So now we are generating summaries even

677
00:32:36,620 --> 00:32:41,100
at a small size, which are preferred over human summaries

678
00:32:41,100 --> 00:32:43,700
And this is one of the earlier results in RLHF

679
00:32:43,700 --> 00:32:46,260
which shows the power of optimizing

680
00:32:46,260 --> 00:32:49,030
for preferences directly

681
00:32:49,030 --> 00:32:50,740
So the question is like, are there models

682
00:32:50,740 --> 00:32:53,420
for human preferences which are not just binary

683
00:32:53,420 --> 00:32:55,760
and account for fuzzy

684
00:32:55,760 --> 00:33:01,540
I think this is a very hard question, which goes into--

685
00:33:01,540 --> 00:33:04,660
so it is known that human preferences can often

686
00:33:04,660 --> 00:33:06,260
be intransitive

687
00:33:06,260 --> 00:33:08,100
So what do I mean by that

688
00:33:08,100 --> 00:33:12,820
Let's say we prefer A over B. Then we prefer B over C

689
00:33:12,820 --> 00:33:17,900
And then we prefer C over A. This doesn't make any sense

690
00:33:17,900 --> 00:33:20,520
because ideally, if you prefer A over B and B over C

691
00:33:20,520 --> 00:33:22,420
you should prefer A over C. But we

692
00:33:22,420 --> 00:33:26,990
get lots of scenarios where humans prefer A over C as well

693
00:33:26,990 --> 00:33:31,710
And you will, unfortunately, observe some noise in the data

694
00:33:31,710 --> 00:33:34,110
like that where the preference is-- in fact

695
00:33:34,110 --> 00:33:39,830
the preferences we observe in real life are very, very noisy

696
00:33:39,830 --> 00:33:43,430
And I don't think we have a great solution for this

697
00:33:43,430 --> 00:33:46,470
To even ground this, preference learning

698
00:33:46,470 --> 00:33:48,250
is a binary classification task

699
00:33:48,250 --> 00:33:51,590
We're just trying to predict which sample is winning

700
00:33:51,590 --> 00:33:53,490
So it's a binary classification task

701
00:33:53,490 --> 00:33:55,390
So most binary classification, you

702
00:33:55,390 --> 00:33:58,230
can reach an accuracy of like 90%, 95%

703
00:33:58,230 --> 00:34:00,610
But for most of our language model and reward models

704
00:34:00,610 --> 00:34:03,350
we reach an accuracy of like 65% to 70%

705
00:34:03,350 --> 00:34:07,070
So we're barely doing better than chance

706
00:34:07,070 --> 00:34:09,630
But even that is still like enough

707
00:34:09,630 --> 00:34:13,330
to give us pretty large improvements in performance

708
00:34:13,330 --> 00:34:14,750
The question is like, what changed

709
00:34:14,750 --> 00:34:16,510
to make this differentiable

710
00:34:16,510 --> 00:34:19,030
We did not make anything differentiable

711
00:34:19,030 --> 00:34:21,110
That's the reason why we started doing RL

712
00:34:21,110 --> 00:34:25,440
is to be able to take the policy gradient

713
00:34:25,440 --> 00:34:29,800
This estimator, this does not require us to be able to--

714
00:34:29,800 --> 00:34:30,840
yeah

715
00:34:30,840 --> 00:34:32,480
So that just requires the gradient

716
00:34:32,480 --> 00:34:35,520
to be like with the log probabilities of the model

717
00:34:35,520 --> 00:34:39,760
It does not backpropagate into the reward at all

718
00:34:39,760 --> 00:34:41,800
So the question is like, what if the p theta

719
00:34:41,800 --> 00:34:45,020
RL is less than p theta PD

720
00:34:45,020 --> 00:34:48,639
And wouldn't that encourage the model to go in those directions

721
00:34:48,639 --> 00:34:49,659
It's good to know

722
00:34:49,659 --> 00:34:52,199
So this is like-- the expectation is broken down here

723
00:34:52,199 --> 00:34:53,560
into more explicitly

724
00:34:53,560 --> 00:34:55,840
But it's good to know a property about KL divergence

725
00:34:55,840 --> 00:34:58,720
that it's always greater than or equal to 0

726
00:34:58,720 --> 00:35:01,650
I'll leave that as an exercise for you to prove that

727
00:35:01,650 --> 00:35:05,400
So the penalty is always positive

728
00:35:05,400 --> 00:35:08,440
So the question is, are there intelligent ways

729
00:35:08,440 --> 00:35:13,360
to choose prompts such that we get preferences over things

730
00:35:13,360 --> 00:35:15,460
that the model is bad at

731
00:35:15,460 --> 00:35:16,460
That's a great question

732
00:35:16,460 --> 00:35:16,960
Yeah

733
00:35:16,960 --> 00:35:21,640
So there are works which try to look into it

734
00:35:21,640 --> 00:35:23,860
This like touches upon active exploration

735
00:35:23,860 --> 00:35:26,720
You want to get preference signal explicitly

736
00:35:26,720 --> 00:35:29,160
where the model is confused

737
00:35:29,160 --> 00:35:32,080
So I think there's things which touch

738
00:35:32,080 --> 00:35:34,960
upon we want to maximize information gain such

739
00:35:34,960 --> 00:35:38,440
that the model will learn the best

740
00:35:38,440 --> 00:35:40,830
Is there a well-established standard

741
00:35:40,830 --> 00:35:41,540
I don't think so

742
00:35:41,540 --> 00:35:44,480
But this is a good problem to look into

743
00:35:44,480 --> 00:35:47,700
OK, I will start with direct preference optimization next

744
00:35:47,700 --> 00:35:52,640
So there will be a little bit more math, but it's very fun

745
00:35:52,640 --> 00:35:55,640
So we're going to talk a little bit about direct preference

746
00:35:55,640 --> 00:35:57,860
optimization

747
00:35:57,860 --> 00:36:00,720
So the RLHF pipeline I described

748
00:36:00,720 --> 00:36:03,200
some of you might find it complex, or some of you

749
00:36:03,200 --> 00:36:04,340
might find it easy

750
00:36:04,340 --> 00:36:05,840
But in practice, when we try to do

751
00:36:05,840 --> 00:36:10,680
this at very large-scale models, it can be quite expensive

752
00:36:10,680 --> 00:36:17,160
So let's say you're training a hundred billion parameter model

753
00:36:17,160 --> 00:36:19,060
maybe a trillion parameter model

754
00:36:19,060 --> 00:36:22,610
You have to sample every answer and then query

755
00:36:22,610 --> 00:36:24,170
your reward model

756
00:36:24,170 --> 00:36:30,540
And depending on the RL algorithm you use

757
00:36:30,540 --> 00:36:35,050
you have to compute like value functions and other things just

758
00:36:35,050 --> 00:36:36,850
to optimize the model

759
00:36:36,850 --> 00:36:39,310
And you don't have to understand this figure

760
00:36:39,310 --> 00:36:42,130
I put it specifically to scare you guys

761
00:36:42,130 --> 00:36:45,930
So here, there's just like a whole flow

762
00:36:45,930 --> 00:36:49,830
of things that in practice can be fairly hard to get right

763
00:36:49,830 --> 00:36:51,910
And the more parameters you have in the system

764
00:36:51,910 --> 00:36:54,530
the harder it is to get it right

765
00:36:54,530 --> 00:36:58,890
So RLHF can be pretty complex, but--

766
00:36:58,890 --> 00:37:01,950
so when we were looking into direct preference optimization

767
00:37:01,950 --> 00:37:05,450
our goal was to like, can we make this a lot simpler

768
00:37:05,450 --> 00:37:11,490
And yeah, as I said, they have some initial pipeline

769
00:37:11,490 --> 00:37:12,930
which we refer to as RM

770
00:37:12,930 --> 00:37:15,690
We take an instruction-tuned model

771
00:37:15,690 --> 00:37:17,890
and produce a final RLHF model

772
00:37:17,890 --> 00:37:21,480
But if you look at the whole process

773
00:37:21,480 --> 00:37:22,940
we have a language model, and we're

774
00:37:22,940 --> 00:37:24,580
collecting some preferences

775
00:37:24,580 --> 00:37:27,780
Why can we just not directly train on the preference data

776
00:37:27,780 --> 00:37:30,500
and directly optimize the parameter models

777
00:37:30,500 --> 00:37:33,200
Why do we have to learn this intermediate quantities

778
00:37:33,200 --> 00:37:34,980
like a reward model

779
00:37:34,980 --> 00:37:37,260
And why can't we just directly go from preferences

780
00:37:37,260 --> 00:37:38,900
and optimize our language model

781
00:37:38,900 --> 00:37:42,140
So that's really why it's called direct preference optimization

782
00:37:42,140 --> 00:37:47,100
is that we find a way to go from preferences, too

783
00:37:47,100 --> 00:37:48,920
and optimizing the language model

784
00:37:48,920 --> 00:37:52,940
And the way we do it is that at a very high level

785
00:37:52,940 --> 00:37:56,700
we want to be able to write the reward model in terms

786
00:37:56,700 --> 00:37:59,220
of our language model itself

787
00:37:59,220 --> 00:38:01,060
Why do we want to do that

788
00:38:01,060 --> 00:38:04,940
Because we fit our reward model to the preference data

789
00:38:04,940 --> 00:38:06,620
through a fairly simple classification

790
00:38:06,620 --> 00:38:08,900
loss, the Bradley-Terry loss we discussed

791
00:38:08,900 --> 00:38:11,940
If we can just connect the reward model and the language

792
00:38:11,940 --> 00:38:15,040
model, instead of having a separate process

793
00:38:15,040 --> 00:38:17,100
we can just take the language model parameters

794
00:38:17,100 --> 00:38:20,060
and fit it through the loss directly

795
00:38:20,060 --> 00:38:23,180
And that's really the high level motivation for direct preference

796
00:38:23,180 --> 00:38:25,380
optimization

797
00:38:25,380 --> 00:38:26,980
Yeah

798
00:38:26,980 --> 00:38:34,040
So this part of the math gets a little bit slightly intense

799
00:38:34,040 --> 00:38:35,790
So I'll go through this slowly

800
00:38:35,790 --> 00:38:36,550
OK

801
00:38:36,550 --> 00:38:41,700
So we discussed the scale-constrained RL objective

802
00:38:41,700 --> 00:38:43,540
in the last few slides, where we want

803
00:38:43,540 --> 00:38:48,260
to maximize the expected reward with some KL penalty, such

804
00:38:48,260 --> 00:38:50,940
that the model should not drift away from the instruction

805
00:38:50,940 --> 00:38:52,780
fine-tuned model a lot

806
00:38:52,780 --> 00:38:56,900
So again, the parameters we're going to optimize is p theta RL

807
00:38:56,900 --> 00:38:59,520
p pre-trained and RM are fixed

808
00:38:59,520 --> 00:39:00,690
And for now, we can--

809
00:39:00,690 --> 00:39:04,740
yeah, we're just assuming some kind of a reward model here

810
00:39:04,740 --> 00:39:06,900
It turns out that this equation actually

811
00:39:06,900 --> 00:39:10,460
has a closed-form solution

812
00:39:10,460 --> 00:39:12,300
And this is not possible if you're

813
00:39:12,300 --> 00:39:15,640
doing just the regular expected reward maximization

814
00:39:15,640 --> 00:39:17,530
But once you add a KL constraint

815
00:39:17,530 --> 00:39:19,950
it turns out that you can write out a closed-form solution

816
00:39:19,950 --> 00:39:21,850
to this equation, which is really cool

817
00:39:21,850 --> 00:39:24,350
It's rare to find such things

818
00:39:24,350 --> 00:39:26,690
And the solution looks something like this

819
00:39:26,690 --> 00:39:29,630
This is the optimal policy, p star

820
00:39:29,630 --> 00:39:31,510
where we're going to compute probabilities

821
00:39:31,510 --> 00:39:34,230
over y hat conditioned over x

822
00:39:34,230 --> 00:39:38,950
And roughly, this optimal quantity says that we want--

823
00:39:38,950 --> 00:39:41,750
ignore the Z (x) for now

824
00:39:41,750 --> 00:39:45,670
We have our pre-trained model, p pre-trained y hat given x

825
00:39:45,670 --> 00:39:48,110
And we're going to multiply the log probabilities

826
00:39:48,110 --> 00:39:52,510
with the exponentiated reward

827
00:39:52,510 --> 00:39:57,770
So very simply, put exponentiated--

828
00:39:57,770 --> 00:40:00,130
so if a sentence has a higher reward--

829
00:40:00,130 --> 00:40:03,150
so if y hat has a higher reward, we're

830
00:40:03,150 --> 00:40:04,830
going to upgrade the probabilities

831
00:40:04,830 --> 00:40:08,910
under the optimal distribution by an exponentiated reward

832
00:40:08,910 --> 00:40:10,350
by beta

833
00:40:10,350 --> 00:40:13,290
High reward, high exponent, higher probability

834
00:40:13,290 --> 00:40:14,770
under the optimal policy

835
00:40:14,770 --> 00:40:16,800
If the reward is low, it's going to be

836
00:40:16,800 --> 00:40:21,640
downgraded by exponentiated reward as well

837
00:40:21,640 --> 00:40:24,880
And Z (x) here is just a normalizing factor

838
00:40:24,880 --> 00:40:28,440
which ensures that this quantity is a probability distribution

839
00:40:28,440 --> 00:40:31,280
So really, you only need to focus on the numerator

840
00:40:31,280 --> 00:40:33,060
And that's it

841
00:40:33,060 --> 00:40:34,480
You'll have to take my word for it

842
00:40:34,480 --> 00:40:39,440
that this is the optimal solution for the above equation

843
00:40:39,440 --> 00:40:42,880
Maybe I can share something later on how to derive this

844
00:40:42,880 --> 00:40:45,080
But yeah

845
00:40:45,080 --> 00:40:45,880
Cool

846
00:40:45,880 --> 00:40:49,000
Now this is where the key observation comes in

847
00:40:49,000 --> 00:40:51,600
is that because we have this optimal solution

848
00:40:51,600 --> 00:40:54,280
to the above equation, we can rewrite the reward

849
00:40:54,280 --> 00:40:58,520
model in terms of the optimal policy itself

850
00:40:58,520 --> 00:41:01,680
So this lower equation is just like a rearrangement

851
00:41:01,680 --> 00:41:03,640
of the terms

852
00:41:03,640 --> 00:41:05,200
The reward model can--

853
00:41:05,200 --> 00:41:09,200
RM (x comma y hat) can be written as beta log p star

854
00:41:09,200 --> 00:41:11,080
upon p PT

855
00:41:11,080 --> 00:41:13,620
And we have some beta log Z (x) term

856
00:41:13,620 --> 00:41:17,520
which is a constant over y

857
00:41:17,520 --> 00:41:22,080
So again, just to make sure this makes sense intuitively as well

858
00:41:22,080 --> 00:41:26,040
if a completion y hat should have a high score

859
00:41:26,040 --> 00:41:29,400
it will typically have a high probability under p star

860
00:41:29,400 --> 00:41:30,480
as well

861
00:41:30,480 --> 00:41:33,400
And p PT is something that's fixed

862
00:41:33,400 --> 00:41:36,240
so beta log will be high whenever the reward

863
00:41:36,240 --> 00:41:39,640
model gives a high score

864
00:41:39,640 --> 00:41:45,360
And similarly, if a reward model assigns a poor score to y hat

865
00:41:45,360 --> 00:41:46,920
our optimal policy will typically

866
00:41:46,920 --> 00:41:50,760
assign a low probability to y hat

867
00:41:50,760 --> 00:41:56,040
And typically, the beta log p star by PT will also be low

868
00:41:56,040 --> 00:41:57,780
So the relationship sort of makes sense

869
00:41:57,780 --> 00:42:00,120
And we can ignore Z (x) for now

870
00:42:00,120 --> 00:42:02,440
Cool

871
00:42:02,440 --> 00:42:07,120
The main argument here is that this isn't just

872
00:42:07,120 --> 00:42:09,720
true for the optimal policy

873
00:42:09,720 --> 00:42:13,650
You can always plug in some reward model p theta

874
00:42:13,650 --> 00:42:18,490
And it will be optimal for some kind of reward model

875
00:42:18,490 --> 00:42:21,970
And that's really like the observation you make here

876
00:42:21,970 --> 00:42:25,330
And yeah, so we've just replaced p star

877
00:42:25,330 --> 00:42:27,190
with some kind of a learnable policy

878
00:42:27,190 --> 00:42:28,970
given that our parameter's theta represent

879
00:42:28,970 --> 00:42:32,930
the optimal policy for some kind of a reward model

880
00:42:32,930 --> 00:42:34,390
And after that is simple

881
00:42:34,390 --> 00:42:37,050
Given the relationship we have derived so far

882
00:42:37,050 --> 00:42:39,250
we know this is how we fit our reward model

883
00:42:39,250 --> 00:42:40,830
through the Bradley-Terry loss

884
00:42:40,830 --> 00:42:43,570
We want to make sure that the reward

885
00:42:43,570 --> 00:42:45,290
for the winning completion is higher

886
00:42:45,290 --> 00:42:49,090
than the reward for the losing completion

887
00:42:49,090 --> 00:42:54,050
And so far, I've been asking you to ignore the partition

888
00:42:54,050 --> 00:42:57,530
function, but that was for a reason

889
00:42:57,530 --> 00:43:00,090
The reason is that because the objective only

890
00:43:00,090 --> 00:43:03,650
optimizes for the difference between the rewards

891
00:43:03,650 --> 00:43:07,730
the partition function Z (x) just simply cancels out

892
00:43:07,730 --> 00:43:13,300
So maybe I can write this out on the board

893
00:43:13,300 --> 00:43:20,100
We started with the relation RM theta x comma yw

894
00:43:20,100 --> 00:43:28,100
And we had beta log p theta yw given

895
00:43:28,100 --> 00:43:42,440
x, where yw is the winning completion, and p PT yw given x

896
00:43:42,440 --> 00:43:48,420
Yeah

897
00:43:48,420 --> 00:43:51,980
So this is the relation we used with beta log Z (x)

898
00:43:51,980 --> 00:43:55,140
And we're going to rewrite the same thing

899
00:43:55,140 --> 00:43:57,600
for the losing completion as well

900
00:43:57,600 --> 00:44:03,460
So x comma y L beta log P of theta

901
00:44:03,460 --> 00:44:05,500
which is the current parameters we're optimizing

902
00:44:05,500 --> 00:44:07,300
y L is our losing completion conditioned

903
00:44:07,300 --> 00:44:11,660
on the input x divided by the p, the log probabilities

904
00:44:11,660 --> 00:44:16,280
under the pre-trained model, y L conditioned of x and beta log Z

905
00:44:16,280 --> 00:44:20,460
(x)

906
00:44:20,460 --> 00:44:22,920
Now notice that the reward model is like--

907
00:44:22,920 --> 00:44:24,840
the input is the same to the reward model

908
00:44:24,840 --> 00:44:27,440
So we're using the same text, and we're

909
00:44:27,440 --> 00:44:28,520
summarizing the same text

910
00:44:28,520 --> 00:44:30,260
So the input is actually the same

911
00:44:30,260 --> 00:44:33,700
So when we're going to subtract this

912
00:44:33,700 --> 00:44:36,040
we're going to subtract these and this

913
00:44:36,040 --> 00:44:39,180
And this is just simply going to cancel out

914
00:44:39,180 --> 00:44:41,220
The reason why canceling out of this is important

915
00:44:41,220 --> 00:44:43,920
is that Z (x) is actually intractable to compute

916
00:44:43,920 --> 00:44:46,620
It's not something we can compute for real

917
00:44:46,620 --> 00:44:51,460
So we get this relation that you can see over here

918
00:44:51,460 --> 00:44:54,940
is the difference between the reward models is simply

919
00:44:54,940 --> 00:45:01,940
the difference between the beta log ratios here

920
00:45:01,940 --> 00:45:02,780
Cool

921
00:45:02,780 --> 00:45:06,680
So taking this identity which we have just derived

922
00:45:06,680 --> 00:45:10,910
we simply plug it into the original Bradley-Terry loss

923
00:45:10,910 --> 00:45:13,550
And we get something super nice, is

924
00:45:13,550 --> 00:45:19,210
that we will have a data set of, again, input, winning output

925
00:45:19,210 --> 00:45:20,510
losing output

926
00:45:20,510 --> 00:45:23,190
And we're going to optimize this objective directly

927
00:45:23,190 --> 00:45:25,950
for the language model parameters

928
00:45:25,950 --> 00:45:28,750
And this is a classification objective, by the way

929
00:45:28,750 --> 00:45:32,190
So we're not doing any RL loop anymore

930
00:45:32,190 --> 00:45:35,790
We are going directly from the preference data set

931
00:45:35,790 --> 00:45:39,430
and optimizing the language parameter models themselves

932
00:45:39,430 --> 00:45:39,950
Yes

933
00:45:39,950 --> 00:45:41,690
So initially, it will be the same

934
00:45:41,690 --> 00:45:44,110
but the gradient would only backpropagate

935
00:45:44,110 --> 00:45:47,190
through the P thetas

936
00:45:47,190 --> 00:45:50,190
p PT will remain the same throughout

937
00:45:50,190 --> 00:45:52,750
and it will increase the probability of the winning

938
00:45:52,750 --> 00:45:54,990
completion and decrease the probability

939
00:45:54,990 --> 00:45:57,390
of the losing completion

940
00:45:57,390 --> 00:46:01,950
So let's spend a little bit of time here again

941
00:46:01,950 --> 00:46:05,190
This objective will try to encourage the RM theta

942
00:46:05,190 --> 00:46:07,830
for the winning completion to go up and RM

943
00:46:07,830 --> 00:46:11,760
theta for the losing completion to go down

944
00:46:11,760 --> 00:46:15,880
The way to increase RM theta is to increase the probability of y

945
00:46:15,880 --> 00:46:18,960
w under the current model, and decrease

946
00:46:18,960 --> 00:46:22,080
the probability of the losing one under the current model

947
00:46:22,080 --> 00:46:23,680
It's a very intuitive objective

948
00:46:23,680 --> 00:46:24,580
Good thing go up

949
00:46:24,580 --> 00:46:26,440
Bad thing go down

950
00:46:26,440 --> 00:46:31,240
And apparently, you can do it without RL or any complexity

951
00:46:31,240 --> 00:46:36,160
So the question is, can we do something like distillation

952
00:46:36,160 --> 00:46:37,530
instead of this

953
00:46:37,530 --> 00:46:42,200
The question is like, so for the reward model

954
00:46:42,200 --> 00:46:46,480
you first have to get a reward model somehow, right

955
00:46:46,480 --> 00:46:48,440
There's no easy way to get it, so you

956
00:46:48,440 --> 00:46:50,520
have to collect some kind of preference data

957
00:46:50,520 --> 00:46:53,260
to even begin with to get a reward model

958
00:46:53,260 --> 00:46:55,200
And that's where DPO comes in, is

959
00:46:55,200 --> 00:46:58,360
that if you have collected a preference data, instead

960
00:46:58,360 --> 00:47:00,140
of fitting an explicit reward model

961
00:47:00,140 --> 00:47:03,960
you can optimize your language model parameters directly

962
00:47:03,960 --> 00:47:05,040
Yeah

963
00:47:05,040 --> 00:47:08,610
So the question is there's a penalty for pre-trained model

964
00:47:08,610 --> 00:47:10,860
We're not drifting too far from the pre-trained model

965
00:47:10,860 --> 00:47:12,460
Actually, this does do that

966
00:47:12,460 --> 00:47:14,340
So this is a good question

967
00:47:14,340 --> 00:47:18,080
So our loss function is set up as a log sigmoid

968
00:47:18,080 --> 00:47:21,480
So one property of the sigmoid is that the gradients vanish

969
00:47:21,480 --> 00:47:23,400
when the difference becomes--

970
00:47:23,400 --> 00:47:25,100
is already sufficiently large

971
00:47:25,100 --> 00:47:27,520
And the difference becomes sufficiently large, like faster

972
00:47:27,520 --> 00:47:32,120
than the-- so the beta parameter actually controls how quickly

973
00:47:32,120 --> 00:47:34,320
the difference becomes large

974
00:47:34,320 --> 00:47:38,280
So if you have a very high beta, even a small discrepancy

975
00:47:38,280 --> 00:47:40,640
would suddenly saturate the sigmoid

976
00:47:40,640 --> 00:47:44,200
and it would not change the parameters a lot

977
00:47:44,200 --> 00:47:47,080
The question is, is the main advantage

978
00:47:47,080 --> 00:47:50,880
of DPO that you don't have to train a reward model

979
00:47:50,880 --> 00:47:53,120
The way I would say it is that you

980
00:47:53,120 --> 00:47:55,040
are training a reward model, because you're

981
00:47:55,040 --> 00:47:56,840
using the same objective

982
00:47:56,840 --> 00:47:59,720
The DPO provides a connection between the reward model

983
00:47:59,720 --> 00:48:02,720
and the corresponding optimal policy for it

984
00:48:02,720 --> 00:48:05,960
So you don't have to do an explicit or a loop

985
00:48:05,960 --> 00:48:09,830
to go from the reward model to an optimal policy for it

986
00:48:09,830 --> 00:48:14,090
You can all-- we have cleverly parameterized a reward

987
00:48:14,090 --> 00:48:19,090
model in terms of the language model itself

988
00:48:19,090 --> 00:48:21,990
So when you fit a reward model to the preference data

989
00:48:21,990 --> 00:48:24,970
you are optimizing the language model itself too

990
00:48:24,970 --> 00:48:29,170
And so the question is, do we still need to do RLHF or not

991
00:48:29,170 --> 00:48:31,490
given this is cheaper

992
00:48:31,490 --> 00:48:33,670
I think it depends a lot on situation

993
00:48:33,670 --> 00:48:37,650
I think there's still some purported benefits to RLHF

994
00:48:37,650 --> 00:48:41,850
because you can expect like some kind of generalization

995
00:48:41,850 --> 00:48:45,090
from the reward model, which allows you

996
00:48:45,090 --> 00:48:49,250
to grade generations which might not have been seen in preference

997
00:48:49,250 --> 00:48:50,410
data

998
00:48:50,410 --> 00:48:52,550
So there's still some benefit to it

999
00:48:52,550 --> 00:48:56,610
So it depends upon your budget and what you want to achieve

1000
00:48:56,610 --> 00:49:00,850
So the question I get is that the KL divergence

1001
00:49:00,850 --> 00:49:03,210
why are we relying on the sigmoid

1002
00:49:03,210 --> 00:49:07,590
when initially the KL divergence was not related to the sigmoid

1003
00:49:07,590 --> 00:49:10,020
[INAUDIBLE]

1004
00:49:10,020 --> 00:49:12,060
To be more clear or precise, I think

1005
00:49:12,060 --> 00:49:15,820
it's the beta term which controls how fast, how much

1006
00:49:15,820 --> 00:49:18,340
you're allowed to drift away

1007
00:49:18,340 --> 00:49:22,180
And even in the original RL objective

1008
00:49:22,180 --> 00:49:29,180
this beta term is what controls the allowed divergence

1009
00:49:29,180 --> 00:49:31,720
between our current model and the pre-trained model

1010
00:49:31,720 --> 00:49:33,740
So if the beta is really high, you

1011
00:49:33,740 --> 00:49:36,260
are not allowed to diverge at all because you'll

1012
00:49:36,260 --> 00:49:38,460
be penalized a lot more

1013
00:49:38,460 --> 00:49:39,820
Yes

1014
00:49:39,820 --> 00:49:42,980
So the beta controls the strength or the weight

1015
00:49:42,980 --> 00:49:44,920
on this divergence

1016
00:49:44,920 --> 00:49:45,880
That's a good question

1017
00:49:45,880 --> 00:49:47,380
Yeah

1018
00:49:47,380 --> 00:49:50,180
So there's two kinds of sampling involved right now

1019
00:49:50,180 --> 00:49:52,740
First, we want to create a preference data set where

1020
00:49:52,740 --> 00:49:54,680
humans label their preferences

1021
00:49:54,680 --> 00:49:56,460
And then during the RL optimization

1022
00:49:56,460 --> 00:50:00,540
itself, we want to sample to get a score

1023
00:50:00,540 --> 00:50:04,040
under some kind of an external or explicit reward model

1024
00:50:04,040 --> 00:50:06,420
And then we score, and then we optimize

1025
00:50:06,420 --> 00:50:09,680
So we are taking care of the second sampling step

1026
00:50:09,680 --> 00:50:12,070
But the first sampling step is still needed

1027
00:50:12,070 --> 00:50:13,280
Yeah, that's a good question

1028
00:50:13,280 --> 00:50:18,250
So when you're doing online RL or some kind of RLHF procedure

1029
00:50:18,250 --> 00:50:20,620
you don't only need the prompts

1030
00:50:20,620 --> 00:50:23,520
You don't need a preference data set

1031
00:50:23,520 --> 00:50:26,940
So typically, the set of prompts which is used during RL

1032
00:50:26,940 --> 00:50:28,900
is larger than the set of prompts you used

1033
00:50:28,900 --> 00:50:30,830
during preference training

1034
00:50:30,830 --> 00:50:32,040
Yeah, that's a good question

1035
00:50:32,040 --> 00:50:35,060
So I think question basically tries to ask

1036
00:50:35,060 --> 00:50:38,060
can we take into account the strength of the preference

1037
00:50:38,060 --> 00:50:41,180
and not just like a binary label

1038
00:50:41,180 --> 00:50:45,180
I think this is a limitation for both RLHF and DPO, I think

1039
00:50:45,180 --> 00:50:50,040
And we should-- so giving more insight, typically

1040
00:50:50,040 --> 00:50:52,640
when we collect preference data sets

1041
00:50:52,640 --> 00:50:56,120
we ask annotators to give a strength for their preferences

1042
00:50:56,120 --> 00:50:59,140
And we only keep the preferences which are strong

1043
00:50:59,140 --> 00:51:01,820
So typically the preference-- but this

1044
00:51:01,820 --> 00:51:03,450
isn't a resolved problem

1045
00:51:03,450 --> 00:51:07,510
How to learn from noisy data and low preferences or high strength

1046
00:51:07,510 --> 00:51:10,430
preference's still an unsolved problem

1047
00:51:10,430 --> 00:51:13,310
And yeah, these are some of the results for direct preference

1048
00:51:13,310 --> 00:51:15,710
optimization

1049
00:51:15,710 --> 00:51:17,750
We showed like a theoretical equivalence

1050
00:51:17,750 --> 00:51:22,230
between doing the DP objective and doing a KL-constrained RL

1051
00:51:22,230 --> 00:51:23,190
procedure

1052
00:51:23,190 --> 00:51:26,350
And in practice, when we try to look at the summarization task

1053
00:51:26,350 --> 00:51:30,530
and try to measure win rates like we were doing earlier

1054
00:51:30,530 --> 00:51:34,350
we do actually find that it does similar or better

1055
00:51:34,350 --> 00:51:36,450
But this is like a debatable question

1056
00:51:36,450 --> 00:51:37,450
So, yeah

1057
00:51:37,450 --> 00:51:41,230
But in general, it does pretty well

1058
00:51:41,230 --> 00:51:44,910
The beta parameter that--

1059
00:51:44,910 --> 00:51:46,910
do you see this beta parameter here

1060
00:51:46,910 --> 00:51:50,350
That corresponds exactly to the beta parameter in the--

1061
00:51:50,350 --> 00:51:53,110
which is the extra hyperparameter there

1062
00:51:53,110 --> 00:51:56,190
So I'm just going to summarize what we have learned so far

1063
00:51:56,190 --> 00:52:02,960
And then we'll move on to where things are missing and so on

1064
00:52:02,960 --> 00:52:04,880
We want to optimize for human preferences

1065
00:52:04,880 --> 00:52:07,960
because first of all, it's more aligned

1066
00:52:07,960 --> 00:52:09,680
with the objectives we want

1067
00:52:09,680 --> 00:52:12,840
The way we use these models are more closely

1068
00:52:12,840 --> 00:52:14,960
aligned with improving human engagement

1069
00:52:14,960 --> 00:52:17,040
or satisfying human preferences, so we

1070
00:52:17,040 --> 00:52:19,520
want to optimize for that directly as well

1071
00:52:19,520 --> 00:52:22,640
And instead of relying on uncalibrated scores

1072
00:52:22,640 --> 00:52:24,280
from different annotators, we try

1073
00:52:24,280 --> 00:52:27,400
to collect preferences and then learn a reward

1074
00:52:27,400 --> 00:52:30,040
model of some form, either through DPO or through RLHF

1075
00:52:30,040 --> 00:52:33,800
and improve the model through that

1076
00:52:33,800 --> 00:52:37,180
In RLHF, we learn an explicit reward model

1077
00:52:37,180 --> 00:52:40,500
which gives an explicit score for every completion we sample

1078
00:52:40,500 --> 00:52:43,920
And we'll use that to optimize the language model parameters

1079
00:52:43,920 --> 00:52:46,360
And we typically impose some kind of KL constraint

1080
00:52:46,360 --> 00:52:48,760
to make sure that our model does not drift too far

1081
00:52:48,760 --> 00:52:51,920
from where it was initialized

1082
00:52:51,920 --> 00:52:54,600
It's very effective, maybe sometimes even more effective

1083
00:52:54,600 --> 00:52:55,580
than DPO

1084
00:52:55,580 --> 00:52:57,380
But it can be a little tricky to get right

1085
00:52:57,380 --> 00:52:58,800
and it's computationally expensive

1086
00:52:58,800 --> 00:53:03,080
because of the sampling procedure that needs to be done

1087
00:53:03,080 --> 00:53:05,060
And in direct preference optimization

1088
00:53:05,060 --> 00:53:08,040
we can completely skip the sampling part

1089
00:53:08,040 --> 00:53:11,160
and optimize directly on the preference data

1090
00:53:11,160 --> 00:53:15,680
by posing it as a binary classification problem

1091
00:53:15,680 --> 00:53:16,720
Cool

1092
00:53:16,720 --> 00:53:18,400
And it's very simple and effective

1093
00:53:18,400 --> 00:53:21,440
and does not require an online step

1094
00:53:21,440 --> 00:53:23,680
So where does this all lead us

1095
00:53:23,680 --> 00:53:26,200
We've discussed all the RLHF and DPO stuff

1096
00:53:26,200 --> 00:53:30,100
And these are roughly how the chatbots are built, by the way

1097
00:53:30,100 --> 00:53:32,980
So this is the InstructGPT pipeline

1098
00:53:32,980 --> 00:53:36,540
This was one of the first instruction, first chatbots

1099
00:53:36,540 --> 00:53:37,960
which was available to public

1100
00:53:37,960 --> 00:53:40,480
And they interacted with it

1101
00:53:40,480 --> 00:53:43,920
And it worked pretty well

1102
00:53:43,920 --> 00:53:46,220
It can do a lot of tasks

1103
00:53:46,220 --> 00:53:49,360
It can answer general purpose questions

1104
00:53:49,360 --> 00:53:54,680
And yeah, InstructGPT

1105
00:53:54,680 --> 00:53:58,640
This is a comparison between what the pre-trained model gives

1106
00:53:58,640 --> 00:54:01,570
as an answer versus what an instruction even though an RLHF

1107
00:54:01,570 --> 00:54:04,730
model gives us an answer

1108
00:54:04,730 --> 00:54:08,530
And yeah, these are some examples

1109
00:54:08,530 --> 00:54:12,590
And yeah, we have optimized these models for dialogue

1110
00:54:12,590 --> 00:54:14,570
And it's not just summaries

1111
00:54:14,570 --> 00:54:17,610
I want to say we can optimize them for many, many general

1112
00:54:17,610 --> 00:54:18,750
purpose tasks

1113
00:54:18,750 --> 00:54:21,680
And the more data you collect, more tasks you cover

1114
00:54:21,680 --> 00:54:23,350
the better these models are going to do

1115
00:54:23,350 --> 00:54:25,430
the better they're going to understand the intent

1116
00:54:25,430 --> 00:54:31,370
and the better they're going to respond to all of our needs

1117
00:54:31,370 --> 00:54:35,330
And yeah, DPO has been used a lot in the open-source community

1118
00:54:35,330 --> 00:54:37,930
ever since it was launched

1119
00:54:37,930 --> 00:54:39,430
It's fairly cheap, works

1120
00:54:39,430 --> 00:54:40,890
It's pretty performant

1121
00:54:40,890 --> 00:54:48,330
And at some point, all LLMs were using DPO to get their alignment

1122
00:54:48,330 --> 00:54:51,590
with human feedback

1123
00:54:51,590 --> 00:54:53,670
Llama and Mistral models, if you've heard of them

1124
00:54:53,670 --> 00:54:56,210
were also post-trained using DPO

1125
00:54:56,210 --> 00:54:58,380
So, yeah

1126
00:54:58,380 --> 00:55:03,180
And I guess this is an interesting example where it's--

1127
00:55:03,180 --> 00:55:04,780
it's a good question to ask, like what

1128
00:55:04,780 --> 00:55:09,740
is the difference between instruction-tuned model and RLHF

1129
00:55:09,740 --> 00:55:10,560
models

1130
00:55:10,560 --> 00:55:13,380
And one clear stylistic difference that emerges

1131
00:55:13,380 --> 00:55:17,360
is that they tend to give more detailed answers

1132
00:55:17,360 --> 00:55:19,740
well-formatted answers, and they tend

1133
00:55:19,740 --> 00:55:22,180
to align more with the kind of text

1134
00:55:22,180 --> 00:55:24,140
that humans find easier to read

1135
00:55:24,140 --> 00:55:25,660
So the question here are like, what

1136
00:55:25,660 --> 00:55:28,390
are the five most common causes of stress among people

1137
00:55:28,390 --> 00:55:30,140
And if you just do instruction fine-tuning

1138
00:55:30,140 --> 00:55:32,120
with a very small data set, you get a good answer

1139
00:55:32,120 --> 00:55:34,200
The five most common causes of stress among people

1140
00:55:34,200 --> 00:55:36,700
are work, money, relationships, health, and family

1141
00:55:36,700 --> 00:55:39,720
But then you do some kind of a PPO procedure

1142
00:55:39,720 --> 00:55:41,940
You get a much clearer, longer answer

1143
00:55:41,940 --> 00:55:43,920
with a very well-formatted list

1144
00:55:43,920 --> 00:55:45,800
And these are all emergent properties

1145
00:55:45,800 --> 00:55:47,340
This is what the model has discovered

1146
00:55:47,340 --> 00:55:48,680
that humans like and prefer

1147
00:55:48,680 --> 00:55:52,020
And that's why they tend to generate answers of that form

1148
00:55:52,020 --> 00:55:53,440
I love the question

1149
00:55:53,440 --> 00:55:55,880
I'll basically like--

1150
00:55:55,880 --> 00:55:58,560
I had a slide on this towards the end

1151
00:55:58,560 --> 00:56:02,780
But yes, the question is, can we trust like people's feedback

1152
00:56:02,780 --> 00:56:06,100
And what do we do if people don't know what they want

1153
00:56:06,100 --> 00:56:09,560
Again, that's a very hard boundary to go to

1154
00:56:09,560 --> 00:56:12,540
And I think what happens is that there's

1155
00:56:12,540 --> 00:56:14,900
a misalignment between short-term incentives

1156
00:56:14,900 --> 00:56:16,240
and long-term incentives

1157
00:56:16,240 --> 00:56:18,880
There are certain behaviors which look good in short term

1158
00:56:18,880 --> 00:56:21,700
and people give feedback based on short-term incentives

1159
00:56:21,700 --> 00:56:23,940
But sometimes behaviors are--

1160
00:56:23,940 --> 00:56:26,400
these are very undesirable in the long term

1161
00:56:26,400 --> 00:56:31,340
And that is truly a hard problem to solve within the RLHF

1162
00:56:31,340 --> 00:56:32,720
pipeline

1163
00:56:32,720 --> 00:56:33,680
That's a good question

1164
00:56:33,680 --> 00:56:36,940
The question is like, do they rank like two answers

1165
00:56:36,940 --> 00:56:38,840
or multiple answers

1166
00:56:38,840 --> 00:56:43,220
And do we train on nC2 pairs or-- yeah

1167
00:56:43,220 --> 00:56:46,260
So I think yeah, you can roughly like--

1168
00:56:46,260 --> 00:56:48,580
just for efficiency purposes, people typically

1169
00:56:48,580 --> 00:56:50,820
rank more than one answer

1170
00:56:50,820 --> 00:56:53,060
And you get more learning data out of it

1171
00:56:53,060 --> 00:56:56,600
that way because you get nC2 preference pairs

1172
00:56:56,600 --> 00:56:59,380
But you can-- there's something called the Plackett-Luce family

1173
00:56:59,380 --> 00:57:01,550
which is like a generalization of Bradley-Terry that

1174
00:57:01,550 --> 00:57:05,650
allows you to directly train on ranked completions as well

1175
00:57:05,650 --> 00:57:07,670
beyond just preference pairs too

1176
00:57:07,670 --> 00:57:09,410
So you can do either of those

1177
00:57:09,410 --> 00:57:11,630
They probably both work fine

1178
00:57:11,630 --> 00:57:13,170
Yeah, that's a really good question

1179
00:57:13,170 --> 00:57:19,090
So are companies incentivized to give preferences to engagement

1180
00:57:19,090 --> 00:57:21,990
which are not aligned with human

1181
00:57:21,990 --> 00:57:24,330
This goes well beyond the scope of this lecture

1182
00:57:24,330 --> 00:57:27,630
And be like, I don't know what I can say about this

1183
00:57:27,630 --> 00:57:29,830
But we can look back at history

1184
00:57:29,830 --> 00:57:32,920
So this is not an issue that was just true for LLMs

1185
00:57:32,920 --> 00:57:34,590
For example, YouTube had a lot of issues

1186
00:57:34,590 --> 00:57:36,390
with recommendation systems, and people

1187
00:57:36,390 --> 00:57:39,910
were engaging with YouTube way more than they wanted it to

1188
00:57:39,910 --> 00:57:45,350
So in some ways, this is a broader problem

1189
00:57:45,350 --> 00:57:46,990
on how technology should be interfaced

1190
00:57:46,990 --> 00:57:48,810
with people and societies

1191
00:57:48,810 --> 00:57:53,430
But if I briefly recall from that incident

1192
00:57:53,430 --> 00:57:56,480
is that it was actually in YouTube's incentive to make sure

1193
00:57:56,480 --> 00:58:01,640
that the consumers have long-term healthy engagement

1194
00:58:01,640 --> 00:58:03,840
which means if they're like spending

1195
00:58:03,840 --> 00:58:05,960
20 hours a day on YouTube, that's probably not

1196
00:58:05,960 --> 00:58:07,380
good for the product as well

1197
00:58:07,380 --> 00:58:09,510
So they probably want to make sure that they're not

1198
00:58:09,510 --> 00:58:12,440
spending way too much time

1199
00:58:12,440 --> 00:58:15,780
So if you do optimize for long-term engagement

1200
00:58:15,780 --> 00:58:17,840
that should ideally address--

1201
00:58:17,840 --> 00:58:20,480
that should not fall to the sycophantic behaviors

1202
00:58:20,480 --> 00:58:22,960
that we saw from GPT-4, because that

1203
00:58:22,960 --> 00:58:25,440
is more short-term behavior

1204
00:58:25,440 --> 00:58:27,440
But yeah

1205
00:58:27,440 --> 00:58:30,120
Cool

1206
00:58:30,120 --> 00:58:34,500
And yeah, this is basically all we've talked about

1207
00:58:34,500 --> 00:58:39,360
And I wanted to bring up a couple of things that this is--

1208
00:58:39,360 --> 00:58:41,480
let me just jump ahead to the slide

1209
00:58:41,480 --> 00:58:43,740
where-- because we've already talked about it

1210
00:58:43,740 --> 00:58:47,000
is that this is a very fast-evolving topic

1211
00:58:47,000 --> 00:58:49,920
This is touching our lives right now when

1212
00:58:49,920 --> 00:58:55,160
we are deciding how to interface chatbots with humans

1213
00:58:55,160 --> 00:58:59,580
And for people who might not be familiar with this incident

1214
00:58:59,580 --> 00:59:02,680
but GPT-4o became fairly sycophantic

1215
00:59:02,680 --> 00:59:05,040
due to a model update that was pushed

1216
00:59:05,040 --> 00:59:09,560
And suddenly, it was like endorsing all good and bad ideas

1217
00:59:09,560 --> 00:59:14,040
from their users in a way that everyone deemed was unhealthy

1218
00:59:14,040 --> 00:59:18,100
So I don't work at OpenAI, and I'm not making any comments

1219
00:59:18,100 --> 00:59:23,100
But these issues, these are very real

1220
00:59:23,100 --> 00:59:24,600
And we're still trying to understand

1221
00:59:24,600 --> 00:59:28,360
how does our feedback data affect our final model's

1222
00:59:28,360 --> 00:59:30,040
emergent behaviors

1223
00:59:30,040 --> 00:59:33,920
And this affects how we use our products as well

1224
00:59:33,920 --> 00:59:38,140
And yeah, a lot of great questions came up in the class

1225
00:59:38,140 --> 00:59:41,260
And those are active problems people are working on

1226
00:59:41,260 --> 00:59:44,560
And we don't necessarily have the answers to all of them

1227
00:59:44,560 --> 00:59:48,680
So you guys should go look more into it

1228
00:59:48,680 --> 00:59:51,330
Yeah, and the other main thing that I do wanted to touch upon

1229
00:59:51,330 --> 00:59:53,330
which is the limitation of the current paradigm

1230
00:59:53,330 --> 00:59:56,290
is that there's this phenomena of reward

1231
00:59:56,290 --> 00:59:59,890
hacking, which again touches upon misalignment as well

1232
00:59:59,890 --> 01:00:04,290
is that when we train on these learned reward models

1233
01:00:04,290 --> 01:00:06,730
they do align with human preferences

1234
01:00:06,730 --> 01:00:08,310
up to a certain point

1235
01:00:08,310 --> 01:00:10,670
But as you train progressively against them

1236
01:00:10,670 --> 01:00:14,850
they tend to start drifting away from what we truly want

1237
01:00:14,850 --> 01:00:17,770
So it's a very common problem in RL

1238
01:00:17,770 --> 01:00:20,010
when you have proxy rewards instead of true rewards

1239
01:00:20,010 --> 01:00:21,750
that you care about

1240
01:00:21,750 --> 01:00:25,490
And this is generally true for machine learning as well

1241
01:00:25,490 --> 01:00:26,930
So yeah

1242
01:00:26,930 --> 01:00:29,590
This affects our ability to scale reinforcement learning

1243
01:00:29,590 --> 01:00:31,010
too, because if you start hacking

1244
01:00:31,010 --> 01:00:33,650
you have to stop training at some point

1245
01:00:33,650 --> 01:00:37,090
And this is like an active optimization problem

1246
01:00:37,090 --> 01:00:39,890
that people are trying to address

1247
01:00:39,890 --> 01:00:43,910
Yeah, I think the field is evolving quite a lot

1248
01:00:43,910 --> 01:00:46,050
So it goes beyond like--

1249
01:00:46,050 --> 01:00:50,210
people are moving towards more rubric-based things

1250
01:00:50,210 --> 01:00:55,940
where we score them for several different aspects

1251
01:00:55,940 --> 01:00:59,620
But yes, you typically do want to collect many different kinds

1252
01:00:59,620 --> 01:01:03,380
of data to teach the model what kind of behaviors

1253
01:01:03,380 --> 01:01:05,980
we want to instill and what's the right way

1254
01:01:05,980 --> 01:01:08,000
to respond to certain situations

1255
01:01:08,000 --> 01:01:11,380
It's very hard to cover every situation a chatbot might ever

1256
01:01:11,380 --> 01:01:12,300
encounter

1257
01:01:12,300 --> 01:01:15,300
So we are relying on the model to generalize from what kind

1258
01:01:15,300 --> 01:01:16,360
of data we're teaching

1259
01:01:16,360 --> 01:01:19,310
But these things break all the time

1260
01:01:19,310 --> 01:01:21,060
and there are certain unexpected behaviors

1261
01:01:21,060 --> 01:01:23,460
that come out of these models in ways

1262
01:01:23,460 --> 01:01:26,140
where we could not anticipate

1263
01:01:26,140 --> 01:01:28,520
Yeah, I've already touched a little bit upon this

1264
01:01:28,520 --> 01:01:32,580
but it's a very fast-evolving field, very many, many areas

1265
01:01:32,580 --> 01:01:34,660
still to improve

1266
01:01:34,660 --> 01:01:37,160
Preference data is still expensive to collect

1267
01:01:37,160 --> 01:01:39,300
And as we've maybe discussed, it's

1268
01:01:39,300 --> 01:01:43,060
not necessarily representative of what kind of model behaviors

1269
01:01:43,060 --> 01:01:43,560
we want

1270
01:01:43,560 --> 01:01:49,540
So we are looking for better ways to improve the feedback

1271
01:01:49,540 --> 01:01:53,380
I think you listen to a lot more about very cool, evolving things

1272
01:01:53,380 --> 01:01:55,380
in reasoning space, where the models

1273
01:01:55,380 --> 01:01:57,460
are generating their own outputs from

1274
01:01:57,460 --> 01:02:00,220
and are being graded by verifiable rewards

1275
01:02:00,220 --> 01:02:02,220
And that's one of the coolest topics

1276
01:02:02,220 --> 01:02:04,660
we're looking at as well in post-training and reasoning

1277
01:02:04,660 --> 01:02:06,140
as well

1278
01:02:06,140 --> 01:02:10,500
And something that I personally find very interesting

1279
01:02:10,500 --> 01:02:12,460
is that language models are aligned

1280
01:02:12,460 --> 01:02:15,400
to the aggregate preferences of humans

1281
01:02:15,400 --> 01:02:18,200
But we are interacting one on one

1282
01:02:18,200 --> 01:02:21,100
We're not interacting with the entire internet

1283
01:02:21,100 --> 01:02:22,740
So we do want to eventually make sure

1284
01:02:22,740 --> 01:02:24,980
that the models are aligned to our needs and not

1285
01:02:24,980 --> 01:02:26,620
just everybody's needs

1286
01:02:26,620 --> 01:02:29,900
So personalizing language models and having more contextual input

1287
01:02:29,900 --> 01:02:32,780
about us and our preferences is something that's also

1288
01:02:32,780 --> 01:02:35,090
a fairly nice evolving field

1289
01:02:35,090 --> 01:02:37,300
And yeah, we talked a little bit about hallucinations

1290
01:02:37,300 --> 01:02:39,320
and uncontrolled emergent behaviors

1291
01:02:39,320 --> 01:02:43,260
And these are all still active problems we work on

1292
01:02:43,260 --> 01:02:46,210
And that's all for today

